s the exact slice we tighten — happy to show one example and you can judge.","VERIFIED persona/firmo: George Settle is a current Commercial Finance Broker at CFP Group / Commercial Finance Partnership, Tankersley/Rotherham — confirmed on his LinkedIn snippet, the CFP team page, ContactOut and RocketReach. Matches the lead exactly. | VERIFIED packaging model: CFP is a whole-of-market UK brokerage (FCA authorised, NACFB member) carrying every line cited — Asset, Bridging, BTL/HMO, Tax & VAT, Invoice/Cashflow, Commercial Mortgages and Development/Refurb Finance — so multi-lender packaging across 5+ types is structurally real, not a one-off. | VERIFIED specialism fit: public bio describes him doing short-term unsecured Tax/VAT funding plus property, asset, invoice and acquisition finance — exactly the high-velocity, multi-product broker whose intake load Oloxa targets. | NOT VERIFIED (trigger event): the Sheffield 12-unit industrial close, the ~1-month timing, the Affirmative Finance + Reward Funding two-lender stack and the refinance positioning do not appear in any public index; his LinkedIn activity is gated (HTTP 999) and the only crawlable post dates to ~2019. The headline CLOSING signal rests entirely on the recorded (likely scraped) evidence and could not be independently re-confirmed.","Trigger event is unverified — LinkedIn is gated and the deal/lenders/timing surface nowhere public, so the whole 'this week' premise depends on trusting the original scrape; if that post is older than stated, the 'CLOSING/refinancing now' hook is stale and the touch lands flat. | 'CLOSING' is celebratory by nature — a deal-done post is a win brag, not a stated pain; leading with 'I saw you struggled to package this' risks misreading a victory lap. Frame around the next deal / the refi pack, not the one just closed. | Risk of name confusion: several other 'George Settle' profiles and a separate 'George Steele - Equipped Commercial Finance' exist — make sure outreach references CFP/Commercial Finance Partnership specifically to avoid mis-targeting. | Refinance positioning may route to a single long-term lender, which is a lighter packaging job than the multi-lender stack — softer pain on the refi leg specifically (though his broader book is still multi-lender).","voicemail is generic and not anchored to this lead's signal: it references only 'a multi-lender book' with no mention of CFP, his actual product spread, or the CLOSING/development-exit-to-refi trigger. As written it could be left for any multi-lender broker, which the brief explicitly flags as a violation. | sms is generic for the same reason: 'work with whole-of-market brokers on the doc-packaging side' describes who Oloxa works with in general rather than anchoring to George / CFP / the refi moment. It is not tied to THIS lead's signal and could be sent to any broker."
"2","Sway","UK","HIRING","False","unverifiable","LOW","False","Don O'Henly","Commercial Credit Broker","NMS Financial Ltd","don.ohenly@nmsfinancial.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/don-o-henly-46b26731","If Don is in fact growing the commercial-finance desk, a lean NACFB broker scaling on 'quality enquiries' would feel real pain in document intake and lender packaging - chasing accounts, bank statements and asset schedules across multiple lenders before a deal can even be submitted - which is exactly Oloxa's frame, but that pain lives on the NMS Commercial Services side, not the mortgage/protection arm where the only visible hiring footprint sits.","There is no defensible 'this week' trigger: the specific job ad cannot be found live, its standout copy belongs to a different (Leeds) firm, and Don's own LinkedIn activity is login-walled and unverified - so reaching out now would be acting on an unconfirmed, possibly stale or misattributed signal.","LinkedIn connect D0 (human reason only, no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted -> Email D6 (signal + example offer) -> Follow-up 1 email D11 (chase-pain angle) -> Soft call D14 (permission opener) -> Follow-up 2 email/voicemail D20 (commercial-packaging / time-back angle, audit CTA). Lead with LinkedIn/email over phone given the signal is unconfirmed and entity-mismatched - let his reply tell us if the commercial-desk pain is real before investing call effort. If no engagement by D20, pause; this is a low-confidence lead not worth heavier cadence.","Don - came across your profile, the Lloyds/ABL/Savage Silk path into commercial credit broking is a route I don't see often. Always good to connect with people doing development and bridging work on the NACFB side. No agenda, just like keeping a good network in the space.","Don - given you're running commercial credit deals at NMS, I imagine a fair chunk of the grind is pulling accounts, bank statements and asset schedules together before anything can even go to a lender. Different lenders, different document lists, and you're chasing the borrower for the same three things twice. We built something that takes the borrower's uploads, sorts and renames them, flags what's actually missing, and chases the borrower for it automatically - so you only touch the exceptions and the package lands cleaner. Not pitching anything here, just curious how you handle intake today. Worth comparing notes?","Packaging commercial deals at NMS","Hi Don,

Saw you're handling commercial credit at NMS - property, development, bridging, that world.

The bit that always seems to slow those deals isn't the credit call, it's getting a complete document set together. Accounts, statements, asset schedules, and a different checklist for every lender.

We take the borrower's uploads, sort and rename them, flag the gaps, and nudge the borrower for the missing bits automatically. You verify the exceptions, and the lender-ready package comes out cleaner.

Worth me sending a quick example of what that looks like on a bridging case?

Cheers","Hi Don, it's [name] - I know I've caught you out of the blue, so I'll be quick and you can tell me to get lost. I work with commercial brokers on the document side of getting deals lender-ready, and I noticed you're running the commercial credit desk at NMS. Mind if I ask how you're handling borrower doc intake at the minute?","Hi Don, it's [name] - reaching out because I work with commercial brokers on getting borrower paperwork lender-ready faster, with a lot less chasing. No big pitch, just thought it might be relevant to the development and bridging work you do at NMS - give me a call back on [number] if it's worth a quick chat.","Hi Don - [name] here, I work with commercial brokers on the doc-packaging side of getting deals to lenders. Reckon it could be relevant to your bridging/development work at NMS - ok if I send a quick example?","Don - one thing I forgot to mention: the part most commercial brokers tell me actually stings isn't the first document request, it's the second and third chase for the same missing statement while the lender's clock is ticking. That borrower follow-up is the bit we automate, so the deal doesn't stall on you waiting. Happy to show you how it handles a messy intake if useful.","Don - last one from me. The reason I keep coming back to commercial deals specifically: the packaging admin on bridging and development cases is where the days quietly go - building the doc set, the chases, the relabelling - while the lender's clock runs. That's the bit we take off your plate, so the package lands cleaner and you get the time back for the actual broking. Open to a 15-min deal paperwork audit, or I can just leave you the example to look at on your own time?","We already have a process / my admin handles all of this.","Totally fair - most brokers I talk to do have a working system, this isn't about replacing it. The question I'd ask is how many borrower follow-ups it takes to close out a document set, because that's usually where the days quietly go. We just take the sorting and the chasing off whoever's doing it so they spend their time on exceptions, not reminders. If your process is already tight on that, genuinely no need for us.","Person and title are real and current: Don O'Henly is listed as Commercial Credit Broker at NMS Financial Ltd, Newcastle (RocketReach + LinkedIn), ex-Lloyds / ABL Business / Savage Silk - a genuine commercial credit background. | The lean multi-entity ecosystem checks out: NMS Financial Ltd (PRIMIS appointed rep, mortgage & protection, ~12 staff on LinkedIn), Gameplan Financial Ltd (same Silverlink address, PRIMIS protection arm) and NMS Commercial Services Ltd (NACFB member, est. 2001, commercial property/development/bridging finance) - same Wallsend/Silverlink address cluster. | The hiring evidence could NOT be corroborated: no live adviser vacancy for any NMS entity is discoverable on Indeed, Reed, Totaljobs, LinkedIn Jobs or the company sites, and the public 'Meet the Team' page (11 people, all mortgage/protection) shows no open-role notice and no commercial-finance staffer at all. | The 'verbatim Oloxa value prop' is almost certainly misattributed: the exact phrase 'focus on advising - not chasing paperwork' traces to an NJR Recruitment ad for a Leeds wealth-management firm, a generic recruiter trope, not to NMS; the 'increasing client demand / quality enquiries' line returns zero NMS-linked hits.","Core hiring signal is uncorroborated - no live job ad found on any board or the company's own site, and the 'Meet the Team' page shows no open role. | The headline 'verbatim value prop' quote ('advising not chasing paperwork') resolves to an unrelated NJR Recruitment ad in Leeds - likely a copy/attribution error in the lead record, which inflates apparent fit. | Entity-mismatch risk: the visible hiring is on the mortgage & protection side (NMS Financial / Gameplan / PRIMIS); Don and the document-intake/lender-packaging pain Oloxa solves sit on the separate NMS Commercial Services entity. | Recency entirely unverifiable - LinkedIn personal profile returns HTTP 999 (login wall), so we cannot confirm any post-level activity from Don. | Even if real, a 12-person broker hiring one adviser is a thin signal and could be a like-for-like backfill rather than capacity strain.","follow_up_2 makes a capacity/headcount claim that the evidence does not support. The line 'which is usually when brokers start eyeing another hire just to keep up. This is the alternative to that - more throughput without the extra headcount' leans on the HIRING signal, but research states signal_verified=false, recency unverifiable, and confidence LOW. No live job ad was found on any board or the company site, and the only visible hiring footprint sits on the mortgage/protection entities (NMS Financial / Gameplan / PRIMIS), NOT on NMS Commercial Services where Don and the doc-packaging pain actually live. Framing Oloxa as 'the alternative to [a hire]' presumes a capacity-strain narrative the evidence explicitly cannot corroborate. | follow_up_2 persona mismatch. Don is a Commercial Credit Broker = Senior/Commercial Broker persona (frame: less doc chase, cleaner packages, time back). The 'throughput without the extra headcount' angle is the Founder/Owner persona and is misapplied to him."
"3","Ryan","UK","PAIN","True","older","MEDIUM","False","Matt Wood","Commercial Finance Broker","Hallcroft Finance","mattwood@hallcroftfinance.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/matt-wood-3a2368101","As a whole-of-market broker running concurrent bridging, development, MUFB and acquisition cases for repeat investor clients, Matt repeatedly re-collects and re-formats the same borrower/asset documents into bespoke packs for different lenders on every deal - the exact manual document-intake and lender-packaging drag Oloxa removes.","There is no verifiable this-week trigger event - the signal is structural (the nature of his book) rather than a fresh dated event, so a credible 'why now' would have to be manufactured from a generic deal-season or panel-volume angle rather than a real recent moment.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 (once accepted, lead with the repackaging angle) -> Email D6 (signal -> example offer) -> Follow-up 1 email D11 (lender two/three angle) -> Call + voicemail D14 -> Follow-up 2 email/LinkedIn D19 (capacity-without-headcount, offer the one-pager). Lead on LinkedIn since his activity is firewalled and email may be low-confidence; keep cadence patient given the signal is structural, not a fresh trigger.","Matt - came across your profile, fellow traveller in the whole-of-market world. The bridging/dev/MUFB mix at Hallcroft is exactly the kind of book I find interesting right now. Keen to connect and follow what you're up to in Leeds.","Matt - the thing that struck me about a whole-of-market book like yours is how often the same borrower and asset docs get re-collected and re-formatted into a fresh pack for each lender, especially with repeat investor clients running concurrent cases. On the deals I see, that intake-and-repackage step is where the quiet hours go - chasing the missing bank statement, renaming files, rebuilding the pack for lender number three. We've been helping brokers turn that into: borrower uploads once, docs get