"12","Sway","US","CLOSING","True","0-30 days","MEDIUM","False","Robert Meunier","Partner | Senior Loan Officer","Bellevue Capital Group","rmeunier@bellevuecapitalgroup.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/robmeunier","A 5-person brokering bench running 6 simultaneous closings while shopping each deal across credit union, conventional, non-recourse and SBA lenders means the same borrower document set is being re-collected, re-formatted and re-packaged into multiple lender-specific submission formats by hand — exactly the intake-and-packaging bottleneck Oloxa removes.","He is visibly in a high-volume closing stretch (a 2026 close plus the recorded 6-in-6-weeks burst) AND just tried to add a junior broker to absorb load — so this week the constraint is throughput per deal, not lead flow, which is precisely when a packaging-automation pitch lands instead of bouncing.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch, peer angle, correctly framed as the doc-packaging side) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted (multi-lender question, rung-2 example CTA) -> Email D5 (same angle as a question, lets him reply on his time) -> Call D8 mid-morning between closings, with permission opener -> Voicemail + Follow-up 1 email same day if no answer -> Follow-up 2 email D13 (re-trade/volume angle, steps up to the rung-3 15-min audit CTA). LinkedIn-first because the signal is LinkedIn-sourced and he's clearly active there; keep email/call as the warmer second wave, not the opener.","Robert — saw the Puyallup acquisition land, congrats. I work with commercial brokers on the document-packaging side of closings, and the 200+ lender shop model is exactly the setup I find interesting. Would be good to connect.","Robert, congrats on the Puyallup close. Genuinely curious from a peer angle — when you shop one deal across a credit union, a conventional source, and a non-recourse lender, who ends up re-formatting the same borrower docs into each lender's package by hand? That's usually the part brokers running several deals at once quietly dread. We get it close to zero: borrower uploads once, docs sort themselves, missing items get flagged. Worth me sending a quick example?","Multi-lender doc re-packaging","Robert — looks like Bellevue's had a strong close run lately (the Puyallup deal, the 21-day execution).

Quick peer question: when one deal goes out to a credit union, a conventional lender, and a non-recourse source, who ends up rebuilding the same borrower doc set into each lender's submission format?

For most brokers running several closings at once, that re-packaging is the hidden tax — and it's the part nobody really owns.

Where we help: borrower uploads once, docs get sorted and renamed, missing items flagged — so each lender gets a clean package without your team rebuilding it from scratch.

Worth me sending a quick example of what that looks like?","Hi Robert, it's [Name] — I know I caught you out of the blue, can I borrow 20 seconds? I'm not selling a loan product; I work with commercial brokers on the document-packaging side of closings, and I had one specific question about how your team handles multi-lender submissions.","Robert, it's [Name] — I'm not with a lender, I work with commercial brokers on the document side of closings. Wanted to ask how you're handling the same borrower docs getting re-packaged across all those different lenders right now — give me a call back when you get a minute.","Robert — [Name] here, I work with commercial brokers on the doc-packaging side of closings. Saw the Puyallup close, congrats. Mind if I send a quick example of cutting the multi-lender re-packaging work? No pitch.","One more thought, Robert — the part that usually bites isn't the first submission, it's when a deal gets re-traded and you're rebuilding the package for a brand new lender on short notice. That redo is where the hours go. Curious how your team absorbs that mid-deal.","Last one from me. As the deal volume climbs, the doc chase usually doesn't shrink on its own — it just gets spread around. What actually shrinks it is the borrower uploading once and missing items getting flagged before they stall a file. Open to a 15-min deal paperwork audit? I'll point out where the redo work tends to hide.","My processor / team already handles document collection — we have a system that works.","Totally fair, most teams running this volume do have a rhythm. The idea isn't to take anyone's work away — it's that they only touch exceptions instead of re-sorting and re-formatting every file from scratch for each lender. Same people, a fraction of the manual handling, and packages still go out clean. Happy to show you on one of your own deal types so it's concrete, not theoretical.","Identity and ICP fit confirmed: Robert Meunier is a Partner | Senior Loan Officer at Bellevue Capital Group (Bellevue/Seattle WA), confirmed via the firm's own team page, RocketReach and ZoomInfo; career path CBRE - Walker & Dunlop - DSC Capital - Bellevue Capital, ~$1B career closings, multifamily/construction focus. | Broker-side, multi-lender model confirmed: the firm explicitly positions as a loan BROKER (not a direct lender) with 200+ lender relationships, shopping each deal across multiple capital sources ($300K-$100M+), incl. non-recourse executions seen in his own past closing posts. This is the right side of the table for document-intake/packaging pain. | Active, current closing cadence corroborated: a 2026 LinkedIn closing surfaced ($1.835M multifamily acquisition, Puyallup WA), plus the site cites a recent ~$7.5M close in 21 days and '$22M processed last month' — consistent with the recorded six-closings-in-6-weeks burst, though the exact count/range could not be independently enumerated (LinkedIn activity feed is gated). | Hiring signal real: a 'Junior Loan Broker / Commercial Lead Generator' req was posted (UW Career Center); the specific listing now 404s (likely filled or expired), but it confirms recent capacity-add pressure on the brokering team.","Team-size mismatch: recorded evidence says '29-person brokerage' but the public team page lists only 6 named people (CEO, COO, 3 Partners, 1 Senior LO). The 200+ lenders is accurate; the 29 headcount is unverified and possibly inflated — don't quote it back to him. | The exact 'six closings, $1M-$13.1M' figure could not be independently verified — LinkedIn activity is gated (HTTP 999) and only one 2026 close was findable via search; treat the specific numbers as directional, not confirmed. | Closing posts are celebratory (wins), not stated pain — the packaging-bottleneck angle is an inferred hypothesis, so open with a question about multi-lender resubmission load rather than asserting he has a problem. | Title nuance: he is 'Partner' on the site; the standalone 'Senior Loan Officer' on the team page is a different person (Ryan Carter). Address him as Partner to avoid a sloppy-research tell. | Junior-broker req appears to be lead-gen/cold-calling, not ops/processing — so it signals growth ambition more than an explicit doc-handling hire; calibrate the 'you're scaling intake' framing accordingly.","linkedin_connect_note: fabricated peer identity. 'fellow multifamily/construction guy' falsely claims the sender is a broker, which directly contradicts every other asset in the sequence (cold_call_opener and voicemail both correctly say 'I'm not with a lender / I work with commercial brokers on the document side'). This is an unsupported claim and a sloppy-research tell. Also the only anchor is 'trade notes on the market up there,' which is generic chitchat not tied to the doc/package angle. | email_body: presumptuous tone / asserts an unverified problem. 'someone's rebuilding the same borrower doc set into three different submission packages by hand' is stated as fact about his operation. Research red-flag explicitly says the packaging bottleneck is an INFERRED hypothesis and to open with a question, not assert he has the problem (the LinkedIn DM does this correctly with 'who ends up re-formatting...?'). The email must be reframed as a question/hedge. | follow_up_2: overreaches on the junior-broker hiring signal. 'Even with another broker on the bench, the doc chase doesn't really shrink — it just moves desks' presumes the new hire is there to absorb document work. Research red-flag states the req was lead-gen/cold-calling, NOT ops/processing, and signals growth ambition rather than a doc-handling hire. Claim is not supported; soften so it does not assert the hire's purpose."
"14","Sway","US","HIRING","True","0-30 days","MEDIUM","False","Justin Bunch","Senior Vice President, Loan Officer","Ascent Developer Solutions","justin.bunch@ascentds.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/justinbunch","As a balance-sheet lender scaling toward multi-billion annual volume, every new loan forces the ops team to manually order, chase, and reconcile a heavy third-party document stack (title, tax transcripts, credit reports, property profiles, subordination requests) before closing — exactly the intake-and-completeness bottleneck Oloxa removes, freeing processors to clear pipeline instead of collating PDFs.","They are actively interviewing for the two precise document-handling roles (Business Purpose Loans Processor + Loan Documentation Specialist) right now, which means the doc-intake bottleneck is a live, budgeted, headcount-level problem this week — the moment to pitch software that reduces what those hires would otherwise be drowning in.","LinkedIn connect D0 (growth angle, no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted (ops-throughput reframe) -> email D5 (doc implication + example offer) -> call + voicemail D8 -> follow_up_1 email D12 (new product line angle, offer warm handoff to ops) -> follow_up_2 email D18 (scaling-drag angle). Treat Justin as champion: at first sign of interest, ask for the Head of Ops/COO intro rather than pushing him for a buying decision.","Justin — saw AscentDS went from launch to $2B+ originations in about 18 months, plus the manufactured-housing push. That kind of curve is rare to watch up close. Following along, would be good to connect.","Justin — congrats on the run at Ascent, going past $2B with the new MH/infill lines is no small thing. I noticed you've got the Business Purpose Loans Processor and Loan Documentation Specialist roles open, which usually means the third-party doc stack (title, tax transcripts, credit, property profiles, subordination requests) is piling up faster than the team can order and reconcile it. We help lender ops turn that intake into closing-ready packages with way less manual collation, so new hires spend their time clearing pipeline instead of chasing PDFs. Worth comparing notes on how you're handling intake as you scale?","Doc intake as you scale","Justin — saw the two ops roles open (BPL Processor + Loan Doc Specialist), usually a sign volume is outrunning the doc team.

At your loan sizes, every deal drags a heavy third-party stack: title, tax transcripts, credit, property profiles, subordination requests.

We sort and file all of that on intake, flag what's missing, and nudge for the gaps automatically, so your processors verify exceptions instead of collating PDFs.

The point isn't fewer hires, it's letting the ones you're bringing on clear more pipeline.

Worth me sending a quick example of what a cleaned-up package looks like?","Hey Justin, it's [name] — I know I'm catching you cold, do you have 30 seconds? I saw Ascent's hiring on the loan doc and processing side and wanted to ask one quick thing about how the team handles document intake as you scale, then you can tell me to get lost.","Justin, it's [name] — reaching out because Ascent's opening up the processor and loan doc roles, and we help lender ops handle the third-party document stack so those hires can focus on pipeline instead of collation. No pitch, just wanted to compare notes on intake as you scale — [number].","Justin — [name] here. Saw Ascent's hiring on the doc/processing side. We help lender ops get the third-party stack closing-ready with a lot less manual chase. Worth a quick compare-notes?","Justin — one more thought since you're adding the MH/infill line. New product types usually mean a new document checklist your processors have to learn cold, which is exactly where things slow down. We handle the sort-and-flag part so the team isn't reinventing intake per loan type. If ops is the better person for this, happy to be pointed their way.","Justin — last one from me. At $2B+ and climbing, the doc-intake load usually grows faster than the team you staffed for it, and when it does, it tends to show up as slower closings rather than a headcount problem. If that's even slightly true at Ascent, a 15-min look at how your packages come together might be worth it. Either way, congrats on the growth.","We're not a broker shopping lenders — we fund our own deals, so a doc-packaging tool feels like it's built for the other side of the table.","Totally fair, and that's exactly why I'm pitching it differently. The value for a balance-sheet lender isn't shopping a package around, it's internal throughput: ordering, sorting, and reconciling the third-party stack on every deal you fund. Same intake bottleneck, just pointed inward at your ops team instead of out at lenders.","Both specifically-named ops roles are live right now: 'Business Purpose Loans Processor' (JD = order/review/organize third-party docs: title, credit reports, tax transcripts, property profiles, subordination requests; shepherd loans application to closing/funding) and 'Loan Documentation Specialist' (prepares CRE/construction loan docs, resolves discrepancies). ~17 AscentDS roles listed on Glassdoor, plus Loan Processor, Loan Servicing Specialist, Associate Loan Officer. | Verified hypergrowth, and steeper than the recorded note: launched July 2024 (Elliott Investment Management-backed) -> $1B originations by Jul 2025 -> $1.6B by Oct 2025 -> $2B+ by Dec 2025; public loan data (elementix.ai) cites ~$5.7B trailing-12-mo volume at ~$8.1M avg loan size as of Apr 2026. | Justin Bunch confirmed as SVP/Loan Officer at AscentDS across LinkedIn, RocketReach, and the company's own hires announcement; 20+ yrs origination (ex-Genesis Capital, ex-Michael Rand & Associates) — title and identity match the lead record exactly. | Expansion in motion: AscentDS added manufactured-housing/infill financing in Jan 2026 (first 400+ home-site community in TX, late Dec 2025) and inked large facilities ($400M Thomas James Homes, $100M Cypress/LWK) in late 2025 — new product lines and bigger deals load more document-intake on the ops team.","Lender-side, not broker-side: AscentDS is a direct/balance-sheet private lender, so the classic Oloxa multi-lender 'package once, shop to many' value prop does NOT apply — pitch must be reframed as internal lender-ops throughput/document-intake automation or it will miss. | Single-source originator: the value is volume/scaling efficiency, not lender comparison — a broker-centric script will read as off-target to Justin. | Justin is a producer (Loan Officer/SVP) closing deals, not the ops/processing hiring manager; the hiring need lives in the operations function he feeds, so he is an entry point/champion, not necessarily the buyer of an ops-doc tool — may need a warm handoff to a Head of Ops/COO. | Hiring is partly a celebration-of-growth signal, not pure pain; frame the outreach around helping the new hires scale (capacity), not around their process being broken. | Could not independently timestamp exact job-post dates (Glassdoor/Rise/elementix returned 403/redirect) or verify Justin's specific 'closings in last 4 months' via his auth-walled LinkedIn feed — those rest on the recorded evidence; recency is inferred from multiple live, currently-open postings.","follow_up_2 contains an unsupported proof-style claim: ""Most lenders we talk to don't feel the doc-intake drag until they're a few hundred million past where they staffed for it."" This asserts (a) an established base of LENDER customers and (b) a specific behavioral statistic, neither of which is supported by the evidence or the brand's proof points. Oloxa's allowed proof points are only: 7+ hrs saved/deal, 97% classification accuracy, 0 follow-up emails. Worse, the brand and the research both flag that lenders are OFF the core broker ICP (red_flag: 'Lender-side, not broker-side'), so claiming a pattern across 'lenders we talk to' is fabricated social proof. Reframed it as an observation/hypothesis anchored to Ascent's own $2B+ scale rather than an asserted customer stat."
"17","Ryan","US","PAIN","True","unverifiable","MEDIUM","False","Dillon Freeman","Senior Loan Officer","Fidelity Bancorp Funding","dfreeman@fbfloans.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/dillondoesloans","As a bridge/DSCR originator personally carrying several time-sensitive files at once, Dillon absorbs the cost of incomplete borrower document intake — missing or late docs surface mid-deal, after deposits are committed, as re-request loops that threaten close timelines, which is exactly the front-end packaging friction Oloxa removes.","No verified time-bound trigger this week (recency unconfirmed), so 'why now' rests on his evergreen, self-stated doc-completeness pain rather than a fresh event — a rep should glance at his live feed before using any 'saw your recent post' opener.","LinkedIn connect (no pitch) D0 -> if accepted, LinkedIn DM D2 -> email D5 -> follow_up_1 via email D9 -> call + voicemail D12 -> follow_up_2 via LinkedIn or email D16. Hold SMS unless you get a mobile number or he replies warm - don't cold-blast it. Before any touch, glance at his live feed (handle /in/dilloncfreeman); if there's a genuinely recent post on doc completeness, lead the DM with a specific reaction to it instead of the evergreen open - that single move is the biggest lift to reply odds with a target this senior.","Dillon - came across your profile while looking at folks doing bridge and DSCR origination on the West Coast. The doc-completeness side of that world is something I think about a lot. Would be good to connect with someone closer to the deal flow.","Dillon - the thing about bridge and DSCR files is the incomplete-intake risk doesn't show up early. A borrower's missing item surfaces mid-deal, after deposit money is already committed, and now you're running a re-request loop against a close date. I've been working on the front end of that: borrower uploads get sorted, named and filed automatically, missing items get flagged on day one, and the borrower nudges go out without you chasing. Curious how you handle the doc chase on the time-sensitive ones right now - worth comparing notes?","missing docs after deposit","Dillon - quick one, straight to it.

On bridge and DSCR files the expensive miss is the doc that surfaces late - after the borrower's deposit is already committed and the clock's running.

What I work on sits on the intake side: borrower uploads get sorted, renamed and filed, missing items flagged up front, and the follow-up nudges to the borrower go out on their own. You only touch the exceptions.

End result is a clean file before deposit money's at risk, instead of a re-request scramble mid-deal.

Worth me sending a quick example of what that looks like?","Hey Dillon, it's [name] - I know I'm calling out of the blue, can I grab thirty seconds and you tell me to buzz off if it's not relevant? I work on the borrower-document intake side for bridge and DSCR originators, and I figured you'd have a sharp opinion on where files actually go sideways.","Hey Dillon, it's [name] - reaching out about the borrower-doc intake side of your bridge and DSCR files, specifically catching missing items before deposit money's committed instead of mid-deal. No pitch, just want your read on it - give me a ring back at [number] when you get a sec.","Hey Dillon, it's [name] - we connected on the origination side. Mind if I send over a quick example of catching missing borrower docs before the deposit's committed? Curious if it'd be useful on your bridge files.","Dillon - one more angle. It's not just the missing doc, it's the back-and-forth that eats the days you don't have on a bridge deal. The borrower follow-ups can run on their own so you're not the one chasing - some originators end up sending basically zero of those emails themselves. Happy to show you how that piece works.","Dillon - last note from me. Where this tends to matter most is the multi-property files - a DSCR portfolio or a 1031 with a few legs - where the doc list multiplies and one missing item per property adds up fast. If you've got one of those open, that's the cleanest place to see if this earns its keep. Open to a 15-min deal paperwork audit if you're curious.","My borrowers are the problem, not my system - I can't make them send complete docs, and another tool won't change that.","Totally fair, and you're right that you can't change the borrower. The point isn't to fix them, it's to shrink your end of it. The day a borrower uploads anything, the file gets sorted and the gaps get flagged, so the missing item shows up on day one instead of week three. The nudges to the borrower go out automatically until it's complete. You still can't force them - but you find out early and you're not the one doing the chasing. That's the difference between a heads-up and a mid-deal scramble.","Identity and role confirmed across multiple independent sources: Dillon Freeman, CFA, is Senior Loan Officer at Fidelity Bancorp Funding covering the San Diego region (fbfloans.com team + hiring-announcement pages, LinkedIn, RocketReach). CA DRE confirms him as a currently licensed broker associate (lic. 02228774, exp. 03/2028) under FBF Inc. (corp lic. exp. 04/2029). He is a real, active, licensed originator. | Company model confirmed lender-side: FBF is a direct bridge / multifamily / commercial lender (bridge $1M-$20M, up to 80% LTV, rates from ~9.25%) with in-house DSCR and CMBS programs. The recorded note is right — Dillon's doc-completeness POV is the lender/originator view, not a broker shopping multiple lenders. | Content footprint is real and on-brand: active LinkedIn (posts under handle /in/dilloncfreeman; the lead's /in/dillondoesloans is the same person's Linktree/Instagram brand alias) plus Instagram and Linktree as 'dillondoesloans.' Frequent posting about lending is plausible, but I could not independently count posts per week. | Load-bearing specifics NOT verifiable: the 5 named active files (CA Special Use refi, TX QSR bridge, CA DSCR portfolio refi, NV 1031 3-pack) and the exact 'the more thorough a file is upfront, the less surprises pop up once deposit money starts being spent' quote sit behind LinkedIn's login wall (profile/post fetches returned 999/404). Only datable public post found was the FBF hiring announcement (~spring 2024).","Lender/originator-side pain, not broker-side: the POV is mirrored, so an Oloxa pitch framed for brokers who package files to send OUT to lenders may land sideways — his frustration is borrowers sending incomplete docs IN to him. | Recency unverifiable: the specific posts could not be retrieved or dated; the only timestamped public post is ~2 years old (spring 2024). A 'reaching out because of your recent post' angle is risky without a manual live-feed check first. | The cited file list and exact quote are unverified (LinkedIn login wall). They are plausible and consistent with his profile, but the rep should not quote them back verbatim as if independently confirmed. | Profile-handle mismatch (lead says /in/dillondoesloans; his posting handle is /in/dilloncfreeman) is reconciled to the same person here, but worth a 5-second confirm before any automated personalization keys off the wrong URL. | Senior, sophisticated target (CFA + licensed broker, 7+ yrs at Pacific Premier/Axos): low tolerance for generic AI-sounding outreach — a weak or templated touch will burn the contact.","email_body: 'quick one, originator to originator' implies the sender is themselves a fellow loan originator (a peer identity). The sender is an Oloxa vendor/builder of the intake workflow, not an originator. Nothing in the evidence supports a peer-originator identity, and the cold_call_opener itself honestly frames the sender as someone who 'works on the borrower-document intake side' rather than as an originator. The 'broker peer' instruction governs TONE, not the fabrication of a false professional identity, so this is a minor misrepresentation that should be removed while preserving the casual register. Corrected to 'quick one, straight to it.' (Note: follow_up_2's reference to a 'DSCR portfolio or a 1031 with a few legs' was reviewed against the unverified file list in the research; it is framed as a general deal-type example, not asserted as his specific open files, so it stays inside the 'do not quote specifics back as confirmed' guardrail and is NOT a violation. All other axes are clean: zero banned phrases, the word 'AI' never appears, CTA ladder is correct with the rung-3 audit reserved for follow_up_2 at D16 and no calendar links, the lender/intake-side POV is correctly mirrored rather than using the broker-sends-OUT 'lender-ready package' framing, exactly one hedged proof point ('basically zero of those emails') is used, recency is correctly conditionalized in the sequence, and the mechanism is described accurately.)"
"18","Sway","US","PAIN","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","True","Jeff Singleton","Sr Loan Officer","Saxton Mortgage","jeff@saxtonmortgage.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeff-singleton-2303a330","A self-positioned Non-QM specialist shopping each complex file (bank statement, asset depletion, DSCR, P&L-only, jumbo) across a large wholesale panel is juggling a different doc checklist and condition set per lender per program — precisely the multi-lender intake, doc-sorting, missing-item follow-up, and lender-ready packaging pain Oloxa removes, which only compounds as he turns on an advisor-referral channel of steady complex inbound.","His LinkedIn identity has shifted to 'Non-QM Specialist for Financial Advisors' and he is actively building that advisor referral pipeline, meaning he is scaling complex doc-heavy volume right now, which is exactly when per-lender packaging cost becomes the bottleneck between him and more closings.","LinkedIn connect D0 (the Non-QM specialization is a verified profile field, so lead on the channel where the signal lives) -> LinkedIn DM D2 once accepted -> email D5 (slightly different angle, doc sets per investor) -> follow_up_1 email D9 -> cold call + voicemail D12 -> follow_up_2 email/DM D16. Lead with LinkedIn over phone since he's a residential retail LO and the strongest, most verifiable hook is his own headline.","Jeff — saw you've staked out the Non-QM-for-financial-advisors lane. Not many LOs go deep on bank statement / asset depletion / DSCR instead of running agency vanilla. Always up to connect with people working the self-employed-borrower side of the business.","Jeff — noticed you've built your whole thing around Non-QM for advisor referrals, which means most of your files are bank statement, asset depletion, DSCR or P&L-only rather than clean W2 deals. The part that usually gets heavy there isn't the selling, it's that every program and every investor wants its own doc set and condition list, so intake and packaging eat the day file by file. We help brokers on that exact spot: borrower docs come in, get sorted and renamed, missing items flagged, borrower follow-ups go out on their own, and you only touch the exceptions before it goes to the lender. As you turn the advisor channel up and that complex volume gets steadier, worth comparing notes?","Non-QM doc sets per investor","Jeff — your profile reads Non-QM specialist for financial advisors, so I'm guessing most of your pipeline is bank statement, asset depletion, DSCR or P&L-only rather than clean agency files.

The catch with that mix is every program and investor has its own checklist and condition set, so intake and packaging is a different lift on each deal.

We take that off the broker: borrower docs get sorted, renamed and filed, missing items flagged, follow-ups sent automatically, and you just verify the exceptions before it goes to the lender.

Cleaner, lender-ready package without you chasing each file down.

Worth me sending a quick example?","Hey Jeff, it's [name] — I know I'm catching you cold, can I grab thirty seconds and you tell me if it's worth more? I saw you specialize in Non-QM for advisor referrals, so I wanted to ask how you're handling the doc side when every program and investor wants a different checklist.","Hey Jeff, [name] with Oloxa — reaching out because you run Non-QM for advisor referrals and that's exactly the kind of multi-program, multi-investor doc load we help LOs package faster. I'll follow up by email too, but if it's worth a look my number's [number].","Hey Jeff, [name] here — saw you focus on Non-QM for advisor referrals. We help LOs get those bank-statement and DSCR files packaged for the lender with way less doc chasing. Worth me sending a quick example?","Jeff — quick add to my last note. The advisor-referral channel is great until the inbound gets steady and every CPA-letter or P&L file needs its own condition list. The brokers who scale that channel cleanly are usually the ones who stopped doing intake and follow-ups by hand first. Happy to show how that part runs.","Last one from me, Jeff. One thing advisors notice is turn time — if their client's package moves fast and clean, the referrals keep coming. A lot of that speed is just docs being sorted and complete before the lender ever sees them. If it's worth a look, open to a 15-min deal paperwork audit on one of your Non-QM files?","My loan setup or processor already handles the docs — I don't see what this adds.","Totally fair, and we're not trying to replace that seat. The difference is the front of the file: docs sorted, renamed and filed on intake, and borrower follow-ups for missing items going out automatically. So instead of your setup person living in the chase, they're working exceptions only. On a Non-QM mix where every investor wants something different, that's usually where the hours hide — happy to show it on one real file so you can judge.","Identity + role confirmed across independent sources: Jeff Singleton, Sr Loan Officer, Saxton Mortgage (Mission Viejo, CA) — firm loan-officer page (NMLS#215354), ZoomInfo, RocketReach all align on name/title/company. | Non-QM stack is corroborated by Saxton's own Non-QM page naming the exact programs: Bank Statement (12-24mo), Asset Depletion, DSCR, Jumbo, plus 'CPA or P&L statements' and 'a wide range of investors and lenders' — so the asset-depletion/bank-statement/DSCR/P&L/jumbo spectrum is real, not invented. | LinkedIn headline now reads 'Non-QM Mortgage Specialist for Financial [Advisors/Planners]' (surfaced via search index) — a current profile field that independently validates BOTH the Non-QM specialization and the RIA/CFP advisor-referral orientation. | Could NOT independently verify the '20-30 wholesale lenders per file' phrasing or the '12+ posts in 2 months' cadence — LinkedIn activity feed is gated (HTTP 999); these rest on the original capture, not a re-confirmable source.","Residential retail LO, not commercial-finance/CRE broker — Oloxa's strongest ICP. Fit is 6-8 via the Non-QM/self-employed complexity angle, not a 9-10 commercial match. | The most quotable details ('20-30 wholesale lenders', '12+ posts in 2 months') are tool-unverifiable (LinkedIn HTTP 999). Don't build the opener on a specific lender count or a 'saw your post' hook that could be wrong; anchor on the verifiable Non-QM-specialist-for-advisors headline. | Posting recency unconfirmed — '0-30 days' is unproven; default to '1-3 months'.",""
"4","Sway","UK","CLOSING","True","unverifiable","MEDIUM","False","Jimmy Chan","Commercial Finance Broker","CFP Group","jimmy@cfpgroup.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/jimmy-chan-0528a392","As a whole-of-market broker routinely packaging multi-tranche commercial deals plus parallel second-charge and BTL cases across many lenders (Allica, Reward, Somo, Shawbrook), Jimmy re-collates the same borrower financials, bank statements and asset/lease docs into each lender's bespoke pack format by hand - the document-intake and lender-packaging bottleneck Oloxa removes.","The credible hook is his evidently heavy, ongoing cross-lender packaging workload (recent completions within days, deals in the company feed last week) plus a firm scaling past 30 - NOT a dated 'GBP10M this week' event, which could not be timestamped and should not be cited as fresh.","LinkedIn connect D0 -> LinkedIn DM D3 (once accepted) -> email D5 -> follow_up_1 email D9 -> soft call D11 + voicemail -> follow_up_2 email D16. Lead on LinkedIn since he's active there and not confirmed as the tooling buyer; keep SMS in reserve for warm/referred contact only, never cold.","Hi Jimmy, fellow traveller in the whole-of-market world here. Always good to follow brokers actually placing the trickier commercial and second-charge cases rather than just the vanilla stuff. Following your CFP updates with interest.","Hi Jimmy, your recent commercial flow caught my eye, looks like you're running multi-lender cases pretty constantly. With Allica, the bridging lenders and the rest all wanting the same borrower financials and statements in their own pack format, that re-collation by hand is usually where the hours quietly disappear. We help brokers get docs sorted, renamed and filed automatically, with the missing items flagged before the lender ever asks. Worth me sending a quick example of what a packaged case looks like?","Same docs, every lender's format","Hi Jimmy,

Saw CFP's been busy on the commercial side, and as a whole-of-market shop you're presumably packaging the same case for very different lenders.

The bit that eats the day is reshaping one set of borrower financials and bank statements into Allica's format, then the bridger's, then the next lender's.

We sort, rename and file the uploads automatically and flag missing items before the lender does, so you're left verifying exceptions, not chasing paper.

Want me to send a quick before/after of a packaged case?

Cheers","Hi Jimmy, it's [name] from Oloxa, I know I'm catching you cold so I'll be quick. I work with commercial brokers placing across a lot of lenders, and I noticed CFP's whole-of-market, so I had a hunch about how your cases get packaged. Mind if I take twenty seconds to check whether it's even relevant before I let you go?","Hi Jimmy, [name] from Oloxa, I work with whole-of-market commercial brokers on the document side of packaging cases across different lenders. No need to call back specifically, but I'll drop you a quick note with an example so you can see if it's worth a look.","Hi Jimmy, [name] from Oloxa here, we cut the doc-packaging grind for commercial brokers running cases across multiple lenders. Happy to send a quick example if useful, no pressure either way.","Quick add to my last note, Jimmy: the part most brokers don't expect is the borrower chase. When something's missing, the follow-up goes out automatically, so you're not the one nudging clients for the third bank statement. Happy to show how that runs on a live-style case.","Last one from me, Jimmy. With CFP scaling past 30, the usual instinct is to add a processor to keep up with volume. The angle here is getting more cases packaged cleanly without that headcount step. If it's not a priority right now, no worries, but worth comparing notes if it ever is.","We've got a team for this, packaging is handled in-house.","Totally fair, and the point isn't replacing anyone. It's that even a good processor still hand-reshapes the same docs into each lender's format and chases borrowers for missing items. We take that repetitive intake off their plate so the team spends time on exceptions and judgement calls, which is where they're actually valuable. With CFP growing, it's usually about getting more cases out cleanly without adding the next hire.","PERSON + COMPANY + TITLE CONFIRMED: Jimmy Chan is a real Commercial Finance Broker at Commercial Finance Partnership (CFP Group), based Salford/Manchester - corroborated by his LinkedIn, the CFP team page, a published CFP/Satago broker interview, and RocketReach. CFP is FCA-regulated (ref 912083), NACFB-member, whole-of-market (170+ lenders). | MOST FIRMOGRAPHIC CLAIMS CONFIRMED: Rotherham office is real (4A Fields End Business Park, S63 0JF; opened Jun 2023 per CFP timeline); 'team of 30' confirmed (CFP timeline 'Jan 2025: grew to a team of 30', LinkedIn now ~35); award confirmed precisely as 'Highly Commended - Cashflow Finance Broker of the Year' at NACFB Commercial Broker Awards on 13 Sep 2024; Allica relationship real (CFP was Allica 'Broker of the Month' Jul 2023). | HEADLINE DEAL NOT VERIFIED: No primary source surfaced for the specific GBP10M three-loan Allica refinance or the prior-week Reward (2nd-charge bridge) / Somo (2nd charge) / Shawbrook (BTL) cases. The lender mix is internally credible and broker-recognizable, but the deal reads like an undated LinkedIn deal-post I could not confirm or timestamp - treat GBP10M and the lender list as unconfirmed. | RECENCY PARTIALLY SUPPORTED, NOT FOR THIS DEAL: Jimmy has active recent flow - a Trustpilot/review references a commercial property completion dated 28 May (days before today), and CFP's company feed shows deals '1 week ago'. But nothing dates the GBP10M Allica deal to this week; all dateable accolades are 12-30+ months old (Allica Jul-2023, Rotherham Jun-2023, Award Sep-2024).","Recency of the headline deal is unverifiable - no source dates the GBP10M Allica refinance to this week; opening with 'congrats on the GBP10M Allica deal' risks referencing a stale or misremembered post. | Signal is a CLOSING/throughput celebration, not expressed pain or buying intent - high deal volume can read as 'we cope fine,' not 'we need tooling.' | Headline specifics unconfirmed: the GBP10M figure and the Reward/Somo/Shawbrook lender list did not appear in ANY primary source - do not repeat them as fact; paraphrase the multi-lender pattern instead. | Datable accolades (Allica Broker of Month Jul-2023, Rotherham Jun-2023, Award Sep-2024) are all old and celebratory - leaning on them looks like generic profile-stalking, not a timely reason to talk. | Buyer-fit risk: Jimmy is a producing broker, not confirmed as the ops/tooling decision-maker on a 30-35 person firm (Director Mark Flanagan / ops likely own tooling) - a process-automation pitch may need to reach a principal/ops owner. | Note: 'CFP' is ambiguous online - a US firm 'Commercial Finance Partners' closed unrelated USD deals in 2026; do not conflate it with this UK brokerage in any outreach research.","UNSUPPORTED CLAIM (LinkedIn DM): Names 'Shawbrook' as a lender Jimmy uses. Per research, the Shawbrook (BTL) case is part of the GBP10M deal list that 'did not appear in ANY primary source - do not repeat them as fact; paraphrase the multi-lender pattern instead.' Allica is verified (CFP was Allica Broker of the Month Jul 2023) so it may stay, but Shawbrook must be genericized. | UNSUPPORTED CLAIM (email_body): Names 'Shawbrook' again ('...then the bridger's, then Shawbrook's.'). Same issue - Shawbrook is from the unverified lender list and must be paraphrased, not asserted as fact. | NOTE (not a hard violation, left compliant): The cold SMS asset is retained but the recommended_sequence already correctly reserves SMS for warm/referred contacts only and never cold - keeping it framed that way."
"5","Ryan","UK","CLOSING","True","unverifiable","MEDIUM","False","Bailey Moore","Commercial Finance Broker","Moorgate Finance","b.moore@moorgatefinance.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/bailey-moore-","With an 80+ lender panel and concurrent deals spanning four unrelated sectors, each case requires re-collating borrower documents and re-formatting the pack to a different lender's specific submission template — a manual intake-and-packaging bottleneck that scales painfully as deal volume and broker headcount grow.","A reported multi-sector, multi-lender funding burst plus active hiring is the moment packaging volume outpaces manual process — but because the recency of that burst is unverified, treat 'this week' as a hypothesis to confirm in the opener, not an asserted fact.","LinkedIn connect D0 (anchor to the NACFB award, no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted (panel re-packaging angle) -> Email D5 (signal -> doc implication -> ""send a quick example?"") -> Follow-up 1 email D9 (missing-doc chase angle) -> Call + voicemail D12 -> Follow-up 2 email/DM D17 (capacity-without-headcount angle). If no connect by D5, lead with email instead and keep LinkedIn warm. Confirm the ""why now"" in the opener - do not assert the recent funding burst or hiring as fact; raise them only as questions.","Bailey - saw Moorgate took the NACFB Asset & Leasing gong last year, well deserved. I work around commercial finance doc/packaging workflows and like following brokers running real whole-of-market panels. Happy to connect.","Bailey - the thing that always stands out with a genuine 80+ lender panel like Moorgate's is that the same borrower file gets re-collated and re-formatted to a different lender's template on basically every deal. Across a few unrelated sectors at once, that packaging step quietly becomes the real drag, not the credit work. We help brokers turn the borrower uploads into a clean, lender-ready pack - sorted, renamed, missing items flagged - so the deal goes out tidy the first time. Not pitching a meeting, just curious how you handle the re-packaging across that many lenders right now. Worth comparing notes?","Re-packaging across 80+ lenders","Hi Bailey,

Noticed Moorgate runs a proper whole-of-market panel - 80+ lenders across some pretty different sectors.

Practical upshot of that is every case gets the same docs re-collated and re-shaped to whichever lender's submission format you're going to this time.

We take the borrower uploads, sort/rename/file them, flag what's missing, and hand back a lender-ready pack so it goes out clean first time.

Curious whether the re-formatting per lender is a real time sink on your side, or already sorted.

Worth me sending a quick example?

Ryan","Hi Bailey, it's Ryan - I know I'm catching you cold, can I borrow thirty seconds and you tell me if it's worth carrying on? I work with commercial brokers on the document side, and I noticed Moorgate's running an 80-plus lender panel across a few different sectors.","Hi Bailey, it's Ryan - reaching out about the document collation side of running an 80-plus lender panel, where every deal gets re-packaged to a different format. No urgency, but I've got a quick example that's relevant to how Moorgate works - worth a two-minute look if you're open to it.","Hi Bailey, it's Ryan - quick one re: re-packaging borrower docs across Moorgate's lender panel. Reckon it's worth comparing notes, or are you already sorted on that?","Different angle, Bailey - the part most brokers underrate isn't the first submission, it's the back-and-forth chasing borrowers for the one or two missing items per lender. We flag those upfront and nudge the borrower automatically, so exceptions are the only thing landing on your desk. Is that the bit that eats your week, or is packaging itself the heavier lift?","Last one from me. If you're adding deal flow across that many lenders, the usual instinct is to put another processor on packaging volume - but that just moves the re-formatting work, doesn't shrink it. Worth a look at handling more cases without the headcount step? Happy to walk one of your live case types through it, no slides.","We've already got our own process / our admin team handles the packaging fine.","Totally fair - most brokers running a panel this size already have a system that works. The question is just where the hours go: it's usually the re-formatting to each lender's template and the missing-doc chase, not the credit call. We sit underneath that, so the team verifies exceptions instead of re-collating every file from scratch. If it's already humming, no harm - but I'll send one example against a Moorgate-style case so you can judge it on your own deals.","Firm is real and the credential checks out: Moorgate Finance won NACFB 'Asset & Leasing Finance Broker of the Year' at the 2024 awards (confirmed via nacfb.org), and the homepage states 'Access to over 80 lenders' — confirming the 80+ panel. | Whole-of-market, multi-sector asset finance model is consistent with the recorded GBP 1.32M-across-4-sectors (motorsport/construction/brewery/haulage) deal flow — Silverstone HQ aligns with the motorsport angle. | Multi-lender packaging across an 80+ panel means every deal is re-packaged to a different lender's format — exactly the document-intake/collation surface Oloxa targets. | Could NOT independently verify Bailey Moore's identity/title or date the specific GBP 1.32M funding posts: company site is JS-rendered and LinkedIn is auth-gated. Person-level and recency claims rest on the recorded evidence only.","Recency is unverifiable — the GBP 1.32M funding window could not be timestamped from public sources, so the signal may be stale; do not lead with the specific figure as if it's fresh. | Recorded note flags posts skew celebration-over-pain: a closing/celebration signal indicates throughput, not stated friction, so it's an inferred pain, not a complained-about one. | Bailey Moore's exact title (broker vs. principal/decision-maker) is unconfirmed — 'Commercial Finance Broker' may be an individual contributor without budget authority over tooling. | Award + anniversary + hiring posts are brand/PR signals, which are weaker buying intent than an operational trigger like a new hire onboarding or a stated process complaint.","follow_up_2 asserts an unverified claim as fact: 'With hiring on the go' presents active hiring as established. Hiring is NOT in the research's verified evidence array — it appears only in why_now/red_flags as an inferred assumption, and red_flags explicitly warns the award/anniversary/hiring posts are brand/PR signals with weak buying intent. Leading a touch with it as fact breaches the 'no claim unsupported by evidence' rule and the instruction to treat the why-now as a hypothesis. Reframed as conditional ('if you're adding to the team / as deal flow grows'). | top_objection.response uses replacement framing ('this isn't about replacing the people'). This centers the exact anxiety the brand bans ('replace your admin') — even negated, a strict reviewer flags replacement framing in a cold objection handle. Reworded to focus on where the hours go and the exceptions-only model, with no replace/people-removal frame."
"7","Ryan","UK","CLOSING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","True","Mariam Menebhi","Senior Commercial Broker Assistant","Rainstone Money","mariam@rainstonemoney.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariam-menebhi-12b441156","As the named commercial broker assistant at a 100+ lender brokerage, she is the human re-collating each client's documents into multiple specialist lenders' bespoke packaging templates (Roma, Avamore, InterBay, Atom) deal-by-deal - the exact intake-and-packaging bottleneck Oloxa removes.","A run of recently completed development and semi-commercial deals signals she is at peak packaging volume right now, which is when the manual lender-collation grind is most acute and a packaging-automation pitch lands - but the touch should reference the deal type/role, not quote her specific figures, since their date can't be confirmed.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D2 once accepted (main packaging angle) -> Email D5 (before/after offer) -> Follow-up email 1 D9 (missing-doc flag angle) -> Soft call/voicemail D12 -> Follow-up 2 / SMS if warm D16 (route-to-principals angle). LinkedIn-led because she's most reachable and active there; email and call as reinforcement, not the opener.","Hi Mariam, your name keeps coming up in Rainstone's commercial reviews (the ""went above and beyond"" ones). I work around commercial finance packaging and like following people who actually run the deals, not just post about them. Happy to connect.","Hi Mariam, saw the run of dev and semi-commercial completions Rainstone's had lately, nice work. The bit I'm always curious about with whole-of-market shops is what happens behind those wins: the same borrower file getting re-collated into Roma's layout, then InterBay's, then Atom's, deal by deal. We've been helping commercial assistants take that intake-and-repackaging step off their plate, so docs get sorted and missing items flagged before a pack ever goes out. Worth comparing notes on how you handle the multi-lender side?","Re-packaging for each lender","Hi Mariam,

Saw Rainstone's had a steady run of dev and semi-commercial completions lately.

Behind each one is the part nobody posts about: re-collating the same borrower file into Roma's, InterBay's, then Atom's templates, plus chasing the docs that are still missing.

We take that intake step off the assistant's desk: docs sorted and renamed, missing items flagged, borrower nudges sent, so you're only verifying exceptions before a clean pack goes out.

Worth me sending a quick before/after on one lender's pack so you can see the difference?

Ryan","Hi Mariam, it's Ryan, we haven't spoken, mind if I take twenty seconds and you can tell me to get lost? I work around commercial finance document packaging, and I noticed Rainstone runs a 100-plus lender panel, which usually means someone's re-formatting the same file for Roma, InterBay, Atom and so on. I just wanted to ask how that side currently lands on your desk.","Hi Mariam, it's Ryan, I work around the document side of commercial finance and had a quick thought about the multi-lender packaging on your dev and semi-commercial files. No pitch, just wanted to compare notes, so give me a ring back when you get a sec.","Hi Mariam, it's Ryan, here about the multi-lender packaging side of your commercial deals (Roma/InterBay/Atom reformatting). Happy to send a quick example if useful, no pressure either way.","One thing I forgot to mention, Mariam: the part that tends to land hardest isn't the sorting, it's the missing-doc flags catching a gap before a file goes to the lender, so it doesn't bounce back mid-deal. On a busy run of completions that's usually where the hours quietly go. Want me to show how that flag step looks on a real pack?","Last one from me, Mariam, and happy to leave it here. Since the budget call usually sits with the principals, I'm not asking you to commit to anything, just whether this is worth a look from the person actually doing the packaging. If it's not a priority right now, totally fair, just point me to your next busy stretch and I'll circle back then.","We already have a system and a way of doing this, the team knows each lender's requirements.","Totally, and that lender knowledge is exactly why this works for someone like you, not against you. It doesn't replace how you package, it just does the first pass, sorting, renaming, flagging what's missing, so you start from a clean file and only spend your time on the exceptions and the lender-specific judgement calls that actually need a human. The expertise stays with you; the re-collation grunt work doesn't.","Person-firm tie is independently confirmed: Mariam is named repeatedly in Rainstone's public Trustpilot client reviews on commercial transactions ('responsive, clear, went above and beyond'), and search sources cite her as Commercial Broker Assistant (Manager) - consistent with the recorded title. | Rainstone Money is confirmed broker-side, the correct side for Oloxa: FCA-regulated (No. 743879), NACFB-registered, whole-of-market commercial finance broker in London (E1) that explicitly states 'we are a credit broker, not a lender' with 100+ lenders on panel. | The recorded deal types are corroborated by Rainstone's own public posts of completed deals (e.g. an Instagram '£1,338,500 Development Finance Deal Completed' and a £356,250 semi-commercial completion with InterBay Commercial), so a multi-lender packaging load across Roma/Avamore/InterBay/Atom etc. is operationally real. | The exact recorded figures (£946k dev finance, £280k InterBay refi, £1.28M Atom refi) could NOT be matched in any public source - they appear to come solely from her own LinkedIn deal posts, which sit behind an auth wall and could not be date-stamped.","Recency is inferred, not proven: the headline figures come from gated LinkedIn posts I could not date, so if those deals are older than ~3 months the 'this week' urgency is weaker - lead with the role/workflow, not a stale deal callout. | Do not quote the exact £946k/£280k/£1.28M numbers back to her - they are unverified single-source; misremembering a figure to a packaging specialist who lives in these details would instantly burn credibility. | Completed-deal posts are celebration, not a stated complaint - frame around the packaging grind behind the win, or it reads as congratulations-with-a-pitch. | Title is 'Assistant/Assistant Manager' - she is the workflow champion and clear pain-owner, but the budget likely sits with the principals (CEO Foyaz Ahmed / Director of Sales); treat her as the way in, not the signer.",""
"8","Sway","UK","VOLUME","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","False","Linzi Crellin","Commercial Finance Broker","Halo Corporate Finance Limited","linzi@hcfl.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/linzi-crellin-b3a1b581","A solo broker pushing 200+ deals a year across a 5+ lender panel is the textbook Oloxa pain: she is personally chasing borrower documents (bank statements, accounts, IDs) and re-keying each deal into multiple lenders' bespoke packaging formats, so document intake and lender-ready packaging is the bottleneck that caps how many deals one person can fund.","She publicly posted a fresh 2026 funding milestone (~2 weeks ago per the lead record) and Halo is riding its NACFB Cashflow Broker of the Year win, giving a current, credible reason to reach out this week.","LinkedIn connect D0 (lead with the award, no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D2 once accepted -> email D5 (signal + quick-example CTA) -> call D8 with voicemail if no answer -> follow_up_1 by email/DM D11 -> follow_up_2 D16. Lead on LinkedIn since the signal lives there and she's active; keep email as the proof-drop channel. If she engages on the milestone post, warm the DM off that instead of cold.","Saw Halo picked up NACFB Cashflow Broker of the Year — properly impressive for a working-capital shop. I work around commercial brokers running multi-lender panels, always keen to connect with people doing volume well. No pitch, just good to follow what you're building.","Congrats on the Cashflow Broker of the Year win — that's a serious result. I notice the thing that quietly caps cashflow brokers running a Haydock/Funding Circle/Lenkie/Capify type panel isn't deal flow, it's that every deal gets re-packaged into a different lender's format, with you chasing the same bank statements and accounts each time. We help brokers turn borrower uploads into lender-ready packages with the missing-doc chasing handled in the background, so one person can push more deals without the packaging becoming the ceiling. Worth comparing notes on how you handle re-submissions across the panel?","Packaging across the lender panel","Congrats on the NACFB Cashflow Broker of the Year win.

Running a broad working-capital panel, the bit that quietly eats the day is re-packaging the same deal into each lender's format and chasing borrowers for statements and accounts.

We take borrower uploads, sort and file them, flag what's missing, and nudge the borrower automatically — so you're just verifying exceptions instead of building each pack from scratch.

Usually means a cleaner lender-ready package and a lot fewer follow-up emails.

Worth me sending a quick example of what one looks like?","Hi Linzi, it's [name] — I know I'm calling out of the blue, mind if I take twenty seconds and you tell me to get lost if it's not relevant? I saw Halo won Cashflow Broker of the Year, congrats — I work with commercial brokers on the document and packaging side of running a multi-lender panel.","Hi Linzi, it's [name] — congrats on the NACFB win, calling about the document packaging side of running a multi-lender panel and an idea for getting more deals out the door without more admin. No urgency, I'll follow up by email, but happy to chat if you ring back on [number].","Hi Linzi — [name] here, congrats on the Cashflow Broker of the Year win. Work with commercial brokers on cutting the doc-chasing and re-packaging across lender panels — worth comparing notes? No worries if not.","One thing I forgot to mention — the part brokers tell us bites most isn't the first submission, it's the re-submit when a deal gets declined and has to be reshaped for the next lender on the panel. We keep the borrower's docs sorted and ready so that swap is minutes, not a fresh rebuild. Happy to show how that looks if useful.","Last one from me — for most brokers running a wide panel, the thing that caps how many deals get out the door isn't appetite, it's intake and packaging: chasing docs and reshaping each pack for the next lender. Worth a quick 15-min look at where the paperwork actually slows your deals down?","We've already got our packaging process dialled in — we just won an award for how we run cashflow deals.","Totally fair, and the award says the process works. This isn't about fixing that — it's about who does the manual bits inside it. The sorting, filing, chasing borrowers for the missing statement, reshaping a pack for the next lender. That's still hours a deal whether your process is great or not. We just take that off the person so an award-winning process can run at higher volume without adding a head. Happy to show one example and you can tell me if it's already covered.","Identity + role confirmed independently: Halo's own team page (hcfl.co.uk/about-us/meet-the-team) lists Linzi Crellin as 'Commercial Finance Broker', matching the lead record and the LinkedIn URL. | Firm credibility confirmed: Halo Corporate Finance won NACFB Cashflow Broker of the Year (corroborated by both the NACFB 2025 Commercial Finance Awards winners page and Halo's own post, hcfl.co.uk/news/halo-wins-cashflow-broker-of-the-year/). This is an awarded, high-throughput cashflow/working-capital shop. | Multi-lender stack is exactly the cashflow-broker profile that fits the award (Haydock, Funding Circle, Lenkie, Capify, Flexible = a broad short-term/working-capital panel), meaning every deal is re-packaged and re-submitted across several lenders with different forms and document asks. | Volume + funding specifics (243 deals/yr solo, GBP 3M+ funded in first 5 months of 2026, GBP 174m firm-wide FY2025) come from her LinkedIn activity/profile and could NOT be independently confirmed on the open web — LinkedIn and the NACFB/Halo article bodies were not fetchable; treat these as self-reported, not verified.","Headline volume numbers (243 deals, GBP 3M in 2026, GBP 174m FY2025) are self-reported via LinkedIn and could not be independently verified on the open web — do not quote them back as established fact. | The signal is a celebration/milestone, not a stated pain — risk of pitching into a victory lap; lead with recognition, infer the bottleneck rather than asserting she has a problem. | Exact post date and award ceremony date could not be confirmed (LinkedIn + NACFB pages were cookie-walled/truncated), so the '~2 weeks ago' recency rests on the lead record, not on my verification. | As an award-winning operator she may already have packaging tooling/process in place; frame around scaling solo throughput, not fixing a broken intake process.","follow_up_2 states unverified facts back to the lead as established truth: 'given the volume you're clearly doing solo' relies on the 243 deals/GBP 3M/GBP 174m and solo-operator figures that the research explicitly flags as self-reported via LinkedIn and NOT independently verifiable ('do not quote them back as established fact'). 'clearly doing solo' asserts it confidently. | follow_up_2 is mildly presumptuous and marketer-flavoured: 'the real question is what caps the next jump in deals' frames the conversation for her rather than comparing notes as a peer. | follow_up_2 drifts persona: 'without a hire' is Founder/Owner capacity framing, but Linzi is confirmed as a Commercial Finance Broker (Senior/Commercial Broker persona -> less doc chase, cleaner packages, time back); an individual broker is not necessarily the hiring decision-maker, and the line presumes her staffing situation."
"9","Ryan","UK","CLOSING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","True","Alfie Hall","Commercial Finance Broker","Halo Corporate Finance Limited","alfieh@hcfl.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/alfie-hall-commercial-finance-broker","At 46 brokers pushing GBP 10-13M a month across a 130+ lender panel, the binding constraint is document intake and lender-specific packaging — chasing client paperwork and re-formatting each deal into the right lender's submission — which is exactly the repetitive, error-prone work Oloxa removes.","No hard this-week trigger exists: the freshest dated item is a ~3-week-old single-lender deal-of-the-week post (a celebration, not a pain event), so 'why now' should rest on sustained record-level volume straining intake capacity, not a manufactured event.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 (once accepted; main packaging angle) -> Email D6 (re-anchor + offer example) -> Follow-up 1 email D10 (heavy-doc-product angle) -> Call + voicemail D13 -> Follow-up 2 email/LinkedIn D17 (volume + route-to-ops). Treat Alfie as champion: the goal is a warm intro to ops/director, not a close. Hold SMS unless a mobile surfaces from a reply.","Hi Alfie - came across your work at Halo on the commercial side. Always good to connect with brokers running deals across the full spread (unsecured, bridging, invoice, comm mortgages). Bit of a packaging nerd on my end, so following along with interest.","Alfie - noticed you're running deals across pretty much the whole product range at Halo, from LendingCrowd unsecured through to bridging and commercial mortgages. The bit I always hear brokers grumble about at that spread isn't the deals themselves, it's that every one of those 130-odd lenders wants the borrower docs packaged its own way. We've been working on taking the raw borrower upload and getting it sorted, renamed and filed to a lender-ready package, with the missing items flagged before it goes out. Worth comparing notes on how you handle that across products?","Packaging across 130 lenders","Hi Alfie,

Saw you're arranging deals across the full range at Halo - unsecured, MCA, invoice, bridging, commercial mortgages.

The annoying part at that spread is usually the packaging: every lender on a 130+ panel wants the borrower docs its own way, in its own format.

We take the raw borrower upload, sort/rename/file it, and flag what's missing before the package goes to the lender - so what lands is already lender-ready.

Worth me sending a quick example of what that looks like for, say, a bridging file?

Ryan","Hi Alfie, it's Ryan - we've not spoken, so I'll be quick and you can tell me to get lost. I noticed you're running deals across the whole product range at Halo, and I work on the document packaging side of things for brokers doing exactly that. Mind if I take twenty seconds to say why I called?","Hi Alfie, Ryan here - I work on the borrower-document packaging side for commercial brokers running across multiple lenders, which looks like your world at Halo. No pitch, just had one idea on the per-lender formatting bit that might be worth thirty seconds - give me a call back when you get a sec.","Hi Alfie, it's Ryan - saw you cover the full product range at Halo. Quick one on how you handle borrower docs across all those different lenders. Worth comparing notes? No worries if not.","Hi Alfie - different angle to my last note. The packaging headache tends to bite hardest on the products with the heaviest doc sets, bridging and commercial mortgages especially. If you ever get a borrower who drips files in one at a time, that's exactly the bit we automate the chasing on. Happy to show you how it handles a messy upload if useful.","Last one from me, Alfie. The reason I keep coming back to this: at the volume Halo's clearly running, the doc intake and re-formatting is the bit that quietly eats broker hours - not the deal work itself. If it's more an ops-side call at your end, happy to be pointed to whoever owns that. Otherwise, open to a quick 15-min paperwork audit on one of your live files?","That sounds like an ops or admin decision, not really my call - we've got a client-support team for the paperwork.","Totally fair, and you're right that it'd likely sit with ops to roll out. I'm coming to you because brokers feel the packaging pain first - you're the one waiting on the file before it can go to the lender. If it's useful, I can show you a 2-minute before/after on one product, and if it lands you can point me to whoever owns intake. No commitment on your side.","Verified company + scale: Halo Corporate Finance (hcfl.co.uk, FCA FRN 726655, Cos House 05467304, est. 2005) runs a 130+ lender panel with ~70 staff. The 46-brokers + 22-ops/client-support split is independently confirmed in their 20-year write-up (Business Times), matching the recorded evidence exactly. | Verified high throughput: public figures show October was their 2nd-best month ever at GBP 12.94M to 265 businesses and November ~GBP 10.6M — consistent with the recorded 'best-ever GBP 13.5M / 270 businesses', so the volume claim is credible (treat the exact best-month figure as their internal number, not a quotable web fact). | Verified person + multi-lender packaging surface: Alfie Hall is a real Commercial Finance Broker at Halo, Romford-based, working unsecured loans, MCA, invoice finance, commercial mortgages, bridging and refinance — each lender with its own document set and submission format. He is personally documented arranging a LendingCrowd unsecured loan. | Partly verified: the LendingCrowd relationship is real and public, but the specific ~3-week-old #DealOfTheWeek refinance post could not be found in indexed results (LinkedIn is gated/HTTP 999), so its exact date and 'refinance' framing are unconfirmed.","Mislabeled signal type: this is scale + a celebratory deal post (capability/achievement), not a 'CLOSING' buying trigger or a stated pain. | Wrong-altitude contact: Alfie Hall is an individual-contributor broker, not an owner/ops/tech buyer — purchase authority for an intake/packaging platform is doubtful; the real economic buyer is likely a director or head of operations at the Horsham HQ. Use Alfie as a champion/pain witness, not the closer. | Thin, generic hook: the freshest concrete item is a single-lender (LendingCrowd) win; opening on 'congrats on the panel/volume' is low-differentiation and any vendor could send it. | Do NOT quote the internal numbers back: the GBP 13.5M / 270-businesses best-month figure does not appear in any indexed public source (public sources cite Oct GBP 12.94M/265 and lifetime 22,000+ businesses / GBP 1bn+). Citing an unconfirmed internal figure risks being wrong and burning credibility. | Same-name distractor: 'Halo Business Finance' (US SBA lender) and 'Halozyme/HALO' (US biotech ticker) are unrelated — ensure all outreach references the UK Halo Corporate Finance / hcfl.co.uk only.",""
"10","Sway","US","HIRING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","True","Chris Solinski","Sr. Executive Loan Officer","LitFinancial","csolinski@litfinancial.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/chrissolinski","As a speed-positioned UWM wholesale broker adding processing headcount, the bottleneck is likely document intake and lender-ready file assembly — chasing borrower docs and re-packaging files to each wholesale outlet's stacking spec without blowing the 5/8-day CTC promise.","A recently added processor role suggests they are scaling document-intake capacity by adding people, which opens a credible (if soft) reason to discuss lifting throughput per processor; the trigger is real but not time-sensitive this specific week.","LinkedIn connect D0 -> DM D3 (after accept) -> email D6 -> follow_up_1 (email) D10 -> call + voicemail D13 -> follow_up_2 (email or LinkedIn) D18. Lead the relationship on LinkedIn since the profile is broker-side and verifiable there; keep email as the value-drop channel. Hold SMS unless a mobile surfaces through a reply or warm intro. Aging signal means no urgency framing, space touches out and let the value angles carry it.","Saw LitFinancial's been adding to the processing side. Always interested in how speed-focused UWM shops keep file assembly tight while scaling the team. Figured I'd connect, Chris.","Noticed you brought on processing help recently, makes sense if you're holding a 5/8-day CTC line while volume moves. The usual snag I hear from speed-positioned UWM shops is less the processor count and more the front end: borrower docs coming in messy, then re-stacking each file to whatever the outlet's spec is. We've been helping brokers get that part to lender-ready faster, so a new processor spends time on exceptions instead of sorting and chasing. Worth comparing notes on how you're handling intake right now?","file assembly at speed","Chris, saw you added processing capacity recently.

If you're keeping that 5/8-day CTC promise, the front end is usually where files stall, messy borrower uploads and re-stacking to each outlet's spec.

We help UWM brokers get docs sorted, missing items flagged, and borrower follow-ups handled automatically, so a processor lands on a lender-ready file and just works the exceptions.

Means a new hire lifts throughput instead of grinding on intake.

Worth me sending a quick example?","Hey Chris, it's [name], I'll be quick, this is a cold one so feel free to cut me off. I saw LitFinancial added some processing help recently, and I work with UWM brokers on the doc-intake side of that. Mind if I take twenty seconds on why I called?","Chris, it's [name] calling about the processing side at LitFinancial, specifically how speed-focused UWM shops keep file assembly tight when they add a head. I've got a quick example worth a look, so give me a buzz at [number] or I'll try you again.","Hey Chris, [name] here. Saw you added processing capacity recently, work with UWM brokers on getting files lender-ready faster so new hires hit exceptions not intake. Worth a quick example?","One thing I forgot to mention, the re-stacking piece is usually the hidden tax for wholesale shops, same file repackaged to three different outlet specs. We sort and rename to lender-ready on intake so it's not a manual redo each time. Open to a quick example of what that looks like?","Last note from me, the part brokers tend to like most isn't the sorting, it's that borrower follow-ups for missing docs go out on their own. Takes the chase off your processor entirely. If the doc-chase side is even a minor headache, might be worth a 15-min look at your current intake flow.","I just hired a processor for exactly this, so I'm covered on the doc side.","Totally fair, and that's actually the better setup for this. The idea isn't fewer people, it's that your new processor isn't spending the first hours of every file sorting uploads and re-stacking to each outlet. They land on a clean, lender-ready file and work only the exceptions, which is where the throughput per head and the 5/8-day timeline actually come from. Happy to show a before/after on one file so you can judge.","Chris Solinski is verifiable as a mortgage originator tied to LitFinancial (LinkedIn /in/chrissolinski surfaced in association with the company); LitFinancial is a real mortgage lending entity at litfinancial.com. | Recorded hiring signal is a Senior Mortgage Loan Processor role in Troy, MI (~2 months old) with a Glassdoor-indicated ~$36k base — consistent with a processing-capacity backfill, not a senior revenue hire. | Prior signal: 'looking for experienced loan originators' ~6 months old, indicating an ongoing-but-aging team-growth posture rather than a fresh spike. | Profile is broker-side: UWM wholesale broker, speed-focused (5/8-day CTC claim), packaging loans across wholesale outlets — could NOT be re-confirmed live (site 403, LinkedIn 999, searches rate-limited), so this rests on the recorded evidence.","Aging signal (hiring ~2mo, originator search ~6mo) — no genuine 0-30 day event to justify urgency. | Low-pay processor backfill may be routine ops hiring rather than a pain signal. | Open role is in Troy MI but lead is in Denver CO; Chris may not own that hiring/ops decision. | Could not verify live — litfinancial.com returned 403, LinkedIn 999, and searches were rate-limited; broker/UWM/speed claims rest on recorded evidence only.",""
"11","Ryan","UK","CLOSING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","False","Grant McIntyre","Commercial Finance Broker","CFP Group","grant@cfpgroup.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/grant-mcintyre-2399691ba","With 133 units/£46m GDV of live development finance across 5 sites, a whole-of-market panel including Together, and an in-house packaging/case-management team, CFP is constantly reformatting document-heavy deals to each lender's spec and deadline (land registry, solicitor DD, valuations) — the intake-to-lender-packaging grind Oloxa compresses.","No clean this-week trigger exists: the only dated public event is the firm's £1bn milestone from end of January 2026 (~4 months stale), so a credible outreach should anchor on the live dev-finance/packaging volume rather than manufacture urgency around the milestone.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted -> email D5 (signal + example offer) -> follow_up_1 email D9 (in-house team angle) -> short call D12 with voicemail if no answer -> follow_up_2 email D16 (borrower follow-up angle). Treat LinkedIn as primary since he's an individual broker active there; keep SMS in reserve for warm/owner-mobile only, not this cold sequence.","Grant — fellow traveller in the world of whole-of-market commercial finance (and a Scotsman in Yorkshire, respect). Been following CFP's dev finance work across Yorkshire. Always keen to connect with brokers actually doing the volume rather than just talking about it.","Grant — saw the scale of dev finance CFP is running across Yorkshire, multi-site residential schemes are about as document-heavy as it gets. Every lender on the panel wants the land registry, solicitor DD and valuations packaged slightly differently, and that reformatting tends to land on whoever's closest to the deal. We've been helping brokers turn the borrower-docs-to-lender-ready grind into mostly exception-handling: docs sorted and filed, missing items flagged, borrower chased automatically, you just verify what's actually off. Worth comparing notes on how your packaging side handles the multi-lender reformat?","Multi-lender packaging on dev deals","Grant — CFP's dev finance pipeline across Yorkshire caught my eye, multi-site schemes carry a serious document load.

The bit that eats hours is rarely the deal itself, it's reformatting the same land registry / solicitor DD / valuation pack to each lender's spec and deadline.

We sort and file borrower docs on intake, flag what's missing, and chase the borrower automatically so the deal only surfaces when there's a genuine exception to verify.

End result is a cleaner lender-ready package with a lot less back-and-forth.

Worth me sending a quick example of what one looks like?","Hi Grant, it's [Name] — I know I've caught you out of the blue, can I borrow thirty seconds and you tell me to get lost if it's not relevant? I work with commercial brokers running document-heavy dev finance, and I noticed the multi-site stuff CFP is doing across Yorkshire.","Hi Grant, [Name] here — I had a quick thought on the lender-packaging side of the dev deals CFP runs, specifically the reformatting that happens for each lender. No pitch, just worth a two-minute compare; I'll drop you a note too so you've got my details.","Hi Grant, [Name] here — left you a quick note about cutting the document reformat on multi-lender dev deals. Happy to send a 30-second example instead if that's easier?","Grant — one thing I should've mentioned: this sits alongside an in-house packaging team rather than replacing it. It does the sorting, filing and borrower chasing so the case managers spend their time on the exceptions and the lender relationship, not renaming PDFs. Happy to show how that split works in practice?","Grant — last one from me. The borrower follow-up piece tends to be the quiet win: missing-doc nudges go out automatically, so a deal doesn't stall because someone forgot to chase the third bank statement. The chasing just stops landing on your side. Want me to send the example before I leave you be?","We already have a dedicated packaging function and in-house case managers, so we handle this internally.","Makes sense, and the brokers we work with usually do too. The point isn't to replace that team, it's to hand them deals that are already sorted, filed and chased so they spend their hours on exceptions, lender spec and DD rather than renaming files and nudging borrowers. It tends to make a good packaging team faster, not redundant. Worth a quick example to see where the line would sit for you?","Person + company confirmed real and broker-side ICP: Grant McIntyre has a CFP profile (cfpgroup.co.uk/team_members/grant-mcintyre/) and LinkedIn, is York-based ('a Scotsman adopted by Yorkshire'), email grant@cfpgroup.co.uk. CFP Group is an FCA-regulated whole-of-market commercial finance brokerage (FRN 912083, explicitly 'a brokerage, not a lender'), HQ Ilkley + Rotherham office. | £1bn milestone verified but FIRM-WIDE and dated: multiple sources (incl. a LinkedIn post by CFP's Matt Hawcroft, surfaced ~20 Feb 2026) confirm CFP passed 'over £1 billion in finance brokered' at the end of January 2026 — a company achievement, not Grant's personal deal, and ~4 months old as of 31 May 2026. | Development-finance pipeline corroborated almost verbatim: CFP's published portfolio shows North & West Yorkshire residential of 133 units / £46m GDV across 5 sites (plus 52u/£16m, 43u/£20m, etc.), sourced via Director Paula Flanagan — confirms the recorded evidence but at firm level, not Grant-specific. | Together relationship and packaging load are supported: CFP communications reference a Together partnership (silver tier), and CFP publicly advertises a dedicated 'Packaging' function plus in-house case managers — consistent with high document throughput. The £10M / 35-day refinance with land-registry + solicitor DD could NOT be found online and remains unverified CRM data.","The strongest 'pain' detail — the £10M refinance closing in 35 days with full legal DD — is the ONE element not corroborated online; do not quote that figure/timeline back to him as fact, it may be unverified enrichment. | The £1bn milestone and 133-unit/£46m GDV pipeline are FIRM-LEVEL (tied to CFP and directors like Paula Flanagan/Matt Hawcroft), not Grant's personal book — attributing them to him personally reads as sloppy. | Milestone is a celebration metric AND ~4 months old (end-Jan 2026 vs 31-May-2026); a 'congrats on £1bn' opener is both stale and pain-free for a sophisticated broker. | Grant is an individual broker, not a principal/ops decision-maker; CFP already runs an in-house packaging + case-management team, which sits with directors/ops — he may be influencer-only, and the existing team is a ready 'we already handle this' objection. | Minor data hygiene: recorded location 'York' vs CFP registered offices Ilkley/Rotherham — reconcilable (Grant is York-based) but confirm before referencing geography.","follow_up_2 fabricates an unverified customer-results claim: 'Some brokers we work with have got that down to zero follow-up emails on their side.' The '0 follow-up emails' proof point describes an Oloxa capability (borrower follow-ups sent automatically), not a verified case-study outcome. Attributing a measured 'zero' result to vague 'brokers we work with' is invented social proof and is not supported by any evidence. Reframed as an Oloxa capability rather than a client outcome."
"13","Ryan","US","CLOSING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","False","Michael Bucaro","Senior Loan Officer","Convoy Capital","mbucaro@convoy-cap.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-bucaro-47806859","As a debt-origination loan officer placing net-lease deals with 1031 timing across many state jurisdictions, his recurring pain is the manual grind of collecting and standardizing borrower/property documents (rent rolls, leases, estoppels, entity docs, 1031 exchange paperwork) into clean, lender-ready submission packages fast enough to hit exchange deadlines - exactly the intake-to-underwriting friction Oloxa compresses on the broker/borrower-rep side.","The firm is visibly scaling (new Irvine Towers office, confirmed via CoStar/LABJ) which signals rising deal volume and per-officer packaging load, so reaching out now about cutting document-intake time on the next file is well-timed - lead with the growth/throughput angle (verified) rather than the unverified just-funded deal.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D2 once accepted (growth/intake angle) -> Email D4 (signal -> doc implication -> 7hrs outcome, soft example CTA) -> Follow-up email 1 D8 (follow-up/back-and-forth angle) -> Call + voicemail D11 (permission-based) -> Follow-up email 2 D15 (deadline-risk angle, step up to 15-min audit CTA). SMS only if a warm mobile surfaces — don't cold-blast it.","Michael — saw Convoy's been opening up around SoCal (Irvine Towers, etc.). I work adjacent to debt origination teams on the borrower-package side, so figured I'd connect with someone doing the actual file assembly. No pitch.","Hey Michael — noticed Convoy's been adding offices across SoCal lately, which usually means more files moving through origination at once. The part I hear most about from loan officers in that spot is the intake grind: chasing rent rolls, estoppels, entity docs and exchange paperwork into a clean submission package while the clock's running. We've built a way to get borrower docs sorted, named and filed automatically, with the missing items flagged before they bite you on a deadline. Worth comparing notes on how you handle that today?","borrower docs on the next file","Michael — with Convoy adding offices around SoCal, I figure more deals are hitting origination at the same time.

Usually that means the borrower-doc intake gets heavier: rent rolls, leases, estoppels, entity docs, exchange paperwork — all needing to land clean and lender-ready before the deadline.

We get those uploads sorted, renamed and filed automatically, flag what's missing, and nudge the borrower for it, so you're only verifying the exceptions.

Brokers doing this tend to get back 7+ hours a deal on the package side.

Worth me sending a quick example of what the output looks like?","Hey Michael, it's Ryan — we haven't spoken, this is a cold one, do you have thirty seconds? I'll be quick: I work with debt origination folks on the borrower-document side and noticed Convoy's been expanding around SoCal, so I wanted to ask how you're handling package intake as the volume picks up.","Hi Michael, it's Ryan — reaching out because Convoy's SoCal growth usually means more files in origination at once, and I help loan officers cut the borrower-doc intake time on each one. No rush, but if you want the short version, give me a ring back at [number].","Hey Michael, it's Ryan — quick one re: borrower-doc intake as Convoy scales in SoCal. Mind if I send over a 2-line example of what we do? No call needed.","Michael — different angle than my last note. The bottleneck for most origination teams isn't the first doc request, it's the back-and-forth after: re-requesting the estoppel that came in blurry, the missing entity page, the exchange doc nobody flagged. We automate those borrower follow-ups so they happen without you in the loop. How many rounds does a typical package take you right now?","Last one from me, Michael. When you're juggling files across multiple states and exchange timelines, a missing doc caught late is what blows the deadline. Our flagging surfaces gaps at upload instead of at submission — some brokers run a full package with basically zero follow-up emails. Open to a 15-min deal paperwork audit on a recent file so you can see where the time actually goes?","We already have a process / an assistant who handles document collection, so we don't need this.","Totally fair — most teams I talk to have someone on it, and they're usually good. This isn't about that person at all; it just takes the sorting, renaming, filing and the borrower chase off their plate so they're verifying exceptions instead of assembling every package from scratch. Same person, more files through, deadlines less likely to slip. Happy to show one package side-by-side and you can judge if it's worth anything.","Person + role + location CONFIRMED via multiple live sources (LinkedIn, RocketReach, search snippets): Michael Bucaro is a Senior Loan Officer at Convoy Capital, based in Placentia, CA. A Matthew Bucaro (SVP) also works there - related principals, consistent with a tight-knit debt shop. | Company type CONFIRMED but reframed: convoy-cap.com (CA DRE# 02117282) is a commercial-mortgage / loan-ADVISORY brokerage that arranges debt (bridge + perm) for multifamily, commercial, and net-leased properties by placing borrowers with banks/credit unions/private lenders. Bucaro sits on the origination/capital-markets side - he ASSEMBLES and SHOPS borrower packages, not a net-lease sales/disposition broker. | Expansion CONFIRMED and recent: CoStar ran 'Convoy Capital Opens Office in El Segundo' and LA Business Journal / search results reference a NEW Irvine Towers office as part of an active SoCal expansion - so the firm is in growth mode (more origination volume = more file throughput). Note: El Segundo reads as HQ; the freshest expansion is Irvine Towers, not El Segundo as recorded. | The specific Chicago Heights retail acquisition + simultaneous refi (~1 week ago), the ~8 closings in 7 months across 8 states, and the exact '31-person' headcount could NOT be independently verified - no source surfaced the deal or those figures. Plausible (brokered debt deals rarely get press) but treat as unconfirmed; do not quote these specifics back to him as fact.","The 'CLOSING' trigger (just-funded Chicago Heights deal, ~1 week ago) is UNVERIFIED - no public source confirms the deal or its date. Do NOT congratulate him on that specific closing or cite the 8-state/8-closing/31-person stats as if confirmed; a single wrong detail burns credibility with a debt broker. | Celebration-not-pain risk: a just-funded deal is a relief moment, not active pain. Outreach must pivot to the NEXT file's intake bottleneck, not generic 'congrats on closing' spam. | Segment nuance (now resolved but worth holding): he is DEBT origination at a mortgage-advisory brokerage, not a net-lease SALES broker. Oloxa's pitch must speak to assembling/shopping lender packages (borrower-rep side), which fits - but messaging built for a net-lease investment-sales broker would miss. | Recorded company description ('31-person net-lease CRE brokerage,' 'El Segundo = the expansion') is partly inaccurate - it's a commercial-mortgage debt brokerage and the fresh expansion is Irvine Towers; rep should use the corrected framing to avoid sounding mis-researched.","top_objection.response uses replacement framing ('The point isn't to replace that person'). The Oloxa rules ban the 'replace your admin' angle, and a strict reviewer should not allow any 'replace [the person]' construction even when negated — it still plants the headcount-replacement frame. Persona guidance wants augmentation (more files through the same person, exceptions-only), never replacement. Rewritten to drop the word 'replace' entirely and lead with keeping the person while pulling the grunt work off their plate."
"15","Ryan","UK","CLOSING","True","older","MEDIUM","True","Lisa Eagle","Commercial Finance Broker","ASC Finance For Business","lisa.eagle@asc.co.uk","https://www.linkedin.com/in/lisa-eagle","As the broker who personally 'ensures all documentation is in order' across a ~150-lender panel doing niche multi-stage deals, Lisa is re-keying the same borrower financials into each lender's bespoke intake format — exactly the document-collection and lender-specific packaging grind Oloxa removes.","There is no fresh this-week trigger: the strongest dated proof (NACFB 2024 win) is ~20 months old and is a celebration not a pain event, so a credible 'why now' should be built on her live deal flow / complex-case workload rather than claimed urgency — ideally re-verify a current LinkedIn post before sending.","LinkedIn connect D0 -> LinkedIn DM D3 (once accepted) -> Email D6 -> Follow-up 1 (email) D10 -> Call + voicemail D13 -> Follow-up 2 (email) D17. SMS only if a mobile surfaces and there's warmth. Lead on LinkedIn since the anchor is her own public positioning; no fresh trigger means a slower, lighter cadence reads better than a hard push.","Hi Lisa - came across your profile and the bit about enjoying the niche, complex finance cases stood out. Not many brokers actually like the messy ones. Always good to connect with people who own the harder end of the deal flow.","Hi Lisa - your profile mentions you're usually the one making sure all the documentation is in order, which on complex cases across a panel that size has to be a job in itself. The part I hear most from brokers like you is re-shaping the same borrower financials into each lender's own intake format, deal after deal. We've been helping brokers turn the borrower's upload into a sorted, renamed, lender-ready package with missing items flagged automatically, so the only thing left is verifying the exceptions. Worth comparing notes on how you handle the packaging side right now?","Doc packaging on complex cases","Hi Lisa,

Saw you tend to be the one keeping all the documentation in order on the niche, complex applications - the cases where creative thinking matters but the paperwork still has to be perfect.

On those, the grind is usually re-packaging the same borrower financials into each lender's bespoke format, then chasing the bits that are missing.

We help brokers turn the borrower's upload straight into a sorted, lender-ready package, with the missing items flagged so you only touch the exceptions.

Worth me sending a quick example of what one looks like?

Ryan","Hi Lisa, it's Ryan - I know I'm calling out of the blue, do you have thirty seconds? I came across your profile and that you handle the document side on the more complex cases, and I had one quick question about how you package those for different lenders.","Hi Lisa, it's Ryan - I had a quick question about how you handle the document packaging on your complex multi-lender cases, since that's usually where the admin piles up. I'll drop you a short note too, but feel free to call me back on this number.","Hi Lisa, it's Ryan - left you a quick note about taking the doc-packaging grind off your complex cases. No rush, but happy to send a short example if it's useful.","Hi Lisa - quick follow-up from a different angle. With a panel around 150 lenders, the same borrower file gets re-formatted into a dozen different intake templates. The brokers we work with stopped doing that step by hand and just verify the final package. Happy to show you the before-and-after on a complex case if that's useful?","Hi Lisa - last one from me. Most of the value brokers tell us about isn't actually the time saved, it's that the borrower follow-ups for missing docs go out without them having to remember. On a two-person desk juggling niche files, that's one less thing to drop. Open to a 15-min look at your deal paperwork to see if it'd fit?","Our deals are too niche and complex for software to handle the documents - every case is different.","That's fair, and it's exactly why we don't try to automate the deal itself. The complex thinking stays with you. We only take the repetitive part - sorting, renaming and filing what the borrower uploads, then flagging what's missing - and we hand the exceptions straight back to you to verify. The harder the case, the more of that admin there usually is to clear out of your way.","Lisa Eagle is a real, current commercial finance broker at ASC's Hampshire/Southampton office (LinkedIn lisa-eagle-6208ab144, lisa.eagle@asc.co.uk, ~9 yrs in finance) — confirmed via web check, not just the recorded note. | Her own public positioning maps directly onto Oloxa's pain: she 'particularly enjoys niche, complex finance applications where creative thinking matters' and is described as the one 'ensuring all necessary documentation is in order' — i.e. she personally owns document intake/packaging. | ASC is genuinely multi-lender independent (panel of ~150 lenders, not tied to one bank), and won NACFB Commercial Broker Network of the Year 2024 — both verified via nacfb.org and asc.co.uk; award ceremony was 13 Sep 2024. | The CLOSING anchor is broker-credible (complex multi-stage deals, named lender panel, large refi) but the only verifiable timestamp is the 2024 award (~20 months old); the named deal figures and the ~7-month-old hiring shout could not be individually re-confirmed or dated in this pass.","Primary anchor (NACFB 2024 award) is celebration-not-pain AND ~20 months old — weak as a time-sensitive hook; lead with her complex-deal workload instead. | No evidence is verifiably within 90 days; the GBP 6.19M refi and named-lender deals carry no confirmable date and could be older or firm/network-level rather than Lisa's own. | ASC runs a 20+ office network, so some 'deal' evidence may belong to other brokers — keep personalization to Lisa's own stated focus (complex/niche cases, document accuracy), not specific deals you can't attribute to her. | Very small local team (broker + admin support 'Mia'), so a heavy 'scale your ops' pitch may overshoot; frame around removing repetitive packaging work for a 2-person desk handling complex files. | Confirmed on-ICP (broker-side origination/packaging, multi-lender) — no lender-side or single-lender risk.",""
"16","Sway","US","CLOSING","True","1-3 months","MEDIUM","True","Matthew Beal","Senior Loan Officer","Bellevue Capital Group","matthew@bellevuecapitalgroup.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/matthew-beal-2a886b33","As a broker shopping multifamily refi/acquisition deals across 200+ lenders, Beal re-assembles and reformats borrower document packages to each lender's distinct underwriting checklist on every complex deal - the exact intake/lender-packaging friction Oloxa removes.","The closing signal is real but cooling (most recent datable activity is months old), so this week is a credible nurture/relationship touch on the back of a known busy multifamily pipeline - not a hot just-closed trigger; reach out now before the signal goes fully stale.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D3 once accepted -> Email D6 (repackaging angle) -> Follow-up email D11 (borrower-chase angle) -> Call + voicemail D14 -> Final follow-up email D20 (multi-lender speed angle). Slower cadence on purpose - the closing signal is cooling, so this is a relationship/nurture touch, not a hot trigger. SMS only if he replies warm or shares a mobile.","Matt - came across your profile while looking at multifamily brokers in the PNW. The ex-W&D / HUD background plus shopping mid-market deals across that many lenders is a rare combo. Always good to know people doing the packaging-heavy side of CRE well. Happy to connect.","Matt - the part of your setup that caught my eye: shopping mid-market multifamily across 200+ lenders means the same borrower package gets re-cut to a different underwriting checklist basically every deal. That reformatting and the follow-ups to chase the one missing doc tend to be the quiet tax on a busy pipeline. We help brokers turn borrower uploads into a clean, lender-ready package - docs sorted and renamed, missing items flagged, the borrower nudged automatically, so you're only verifying exceptions. Worth comparing notes on how you handle it now?","Repackaging for 200+ lenders","Matt - saw you run mid-market multifamily at Bellevue, shopping deals across a couple hundred lenders.

That usually means re-cutting the same borrower file to a different underwriting checklist on every deal, plus chasing whatever doc is still missing.

We take the borrower's uploads, sort and rename them, flag what's missing, and nudge the borrower automatically - so what lands on your desk is a clean, lender-ready package and you're only touching the exceptions.

Worth me sending a quick example of what that looks like on a multifamily file?","Hi Matt, it's [name] - I know I'm catching you cold, can I borrow 20 seconds and you tell me if it's worth continuing? I work with multifamily brokers shopping deals across a lot of lenders, and the reason I called is the document repackaging that comes with that - the part where the same file gets reformatted for each lender's checklist.","Hi Matt, [name] with Oloxa - reaching out because you're shopping multifamily across a couple hundred lenders, and I had a specific thought on the package-reformatting side of that. No pitch, just worth a two-minute compare - my number's [number].","Matt - [name] here, the multifamily doc-packaging tool I mentioned. Quick q on how you handle re-cutting borrower files for different lenders - worth a 2-min compare? No worries if not.","Matt - different angle than my last note: the slowest part of a multifamily package is usually the back-and-forth getting a complete file from the borrower before you can even shop it. We handle that chase automatically, so the package is lender-ready sooner. Happy to show you the borrower-side flow if useful.","Last one from me, Matt - the brokers we work with care most about this when a deal needs to go to several lenders fast and each wants the file their own way. One clean source package, re-cut per lender without the manual rebuild. If that maps to how your pipeline runs, worth a 15-min look at your deal paperwork. If not, I'll leave you to it.","I've got a process and a team for this already - my assistant handles the doc collection and packaging.","Totally fair, and the goal isn't to replace that - it's to take the repetitive sorting, renaming, and missing-doc chasing off their plate so their time goes to the judgment calls and lender relationships. Your team still verifies the exceptions; they just skip the grunt assembly. Worth a quick example to see if it actually saves your assistant time, or not?","Person and role confirmed on two independent sources: Bellevue Capital Group team page lists 'Matt Beal, Senior Loan Officer' (15+ yrs CRE debt, $1B+ closed, specialization in multifamily, ex-Walker & Dunlop / HUD), and his LinkedIn (matthew-beal-2a886b33) lists the same firm, Seattle, UW education. | Bellevue is broker-side, not a balance-sheet lender: company site states 'relationships with over 200 lenders,' mid-market CRE $2M-$20M, 'no fees until the loan is approved and funded' - so Beal assembles packages and shops them to many lenders, exactly the multi-lender packaging pattern recorded for Robert Meunier at the same firm. | Active multifamily-closing footprint visible on his LinkedIn: refi and acquisition deals across Seattle/Everett/Renton/Edmonds (e.g. a $5.7M Seattle refi, a $3.435M Seattle acquisition, an Everett 1031 financing), consistent with the recorded Tacoma/Everett/Spokane/Seattle closing pattern. | Datable posts are older than the activity itself: his own 'rate-locked early 2022' closing post decodes to ~Jan 2023, and the firm's 'welcome new Senior Loan Officer' post decodes to ~mid-2024 (his tenure marker). No hard-dated closing in the last 30 days was found - consistent with the recorded note that his closings are 5-7 months old.","Recency is the main weakness: no closing verifiable inside 0-30 days; strongest datable posts are ~Jan 2023 and a mid-2024 hire announcement, so the 'CLOSING' signal is inferred from pattern + older posts rather than a fresh event. | Some of the multifamily deals surfaced on his LinkedIn may be posts he engaged with (Berkadia/Allegro listings) rather than deals he personally closed - do not cite specific deal dollar amounts as his own in outreach. | The cited '96-unit student-housing deal in a tertiary market' and Spokane/Tacoma specifics were not independently confirmed on the public web - treat as unverified; avoid quoting them as fact. | Signal is firm-pattern-led (shared with Robert Meunier at Bellevue) rather than uniquely Beal-led, so it is a softer, lower-urgency touch than a personally-announced fresh close.",""
"19","Ryan","US","CLOSING","True","0-30 days","MEDIUM","True","Chaston Montgomery","Senior Loan Officer","Community Investment Corporation","chaston.montgomery@cicchicago.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/chaston-montgomery-mba-98b30a26","As a single-lender CDFI originator running back-to-back small-multifamily closings (heavy-rehab and ADU/new-construction files), Chaston's bottleneck is assembling complete, committee-ready loan packages quickly and repeatedly under CIC's own underwriting standard — a per-file document-intake and packaging load, not shopping one deal across a panel of lenders.","He publicly posted a fresh closing within the last 1-2 days and another ~3 weeks ago, so he is demonstrably mid-cycle on document-heavy files right now — a credible, current reason to reach out about speeding up intake/packaging while the next batch is in flight.","LinkedIn connect (no pitch) D0 -> LinkedIn DM once accepted, or email if not accepted by D3 -> email D4 -> follow_up_1 (borrower-chase angle) via email D8 -> call + voicemail D11 -> follow_up_2 (fits-your-standard angle + 15-min audit) via LinkedIn or email D15. Hold SMS unless a warm/mobile channel opens up — do not cold-blast it. Lead every touch with the packaging grind, never ""congrats on the close.""","Saw the 6-unit Beverly close land on your feed, right after the 7-unit at 6601 S. Evans. That's a real run of small-multifamily files back to back. I work with loan officers on the document/packaging side of deals like these — figured it was worth being connected.","Two closings posted inside a few weeks — the Beverly 6-unit and the Woodlawn new-construction at 6601 S. Evans. The deals get the spotlight, but the part that quietly eats the days is getting each borrower's file complete and committee-ready: chasing the last few docs, renaming, refiling, making sure nothing's missing before it goes up. I help loan officers tighten that front end — borrower uploads get sorted and filed automatically, missing items get flagged, and the borrower gets nudged for them without you typing the email. Not pitching anything, just curious how you're handling intake when you've got the next batch already in flight — worth comparing notes?","Beverly + Woodlawn back to back","Chaston — saw the 6-unit Beverly close, and the 7-unit at 6601 S. Evans a few weeks back. Steady run of files.
The closings post nice; the part nobody sees is getting each one committee-ready — last docs chased, renamed, refiled, double-checked.
That front end is what I help loan officers tighten: borrower uploads sorted and filed on their own, missing items flagged, borrower nudged for them automatically.
You verify the exceptions instead of assembling the whole stack by hand.
With another batch likely already moving — worth me sending a quick example of what a cleaned-up intake looks like?","Hey Chaston, it's Ryan — I'll be quick, I know I'm catching you cold. I saw the Beverly and Woodlawn deals close back to back, so I'm guessing you've already got the next files in intake. Mind if I take twenty seconds on why I called, and you tell me if it's worth continuing?","Chaston, it's Ryan — saw the Beverly and Woodlawn closings land close together, so you're clearly mid-cycle on a few files right now. I work with loan officers on the document-intake side of getting those packages committee-ready faster, and I think it'd be worth comparing notes — give me a call back when you've got a sec.","Chaston — Ryan here. Saw Beverly and Woodlawn close back to back, nice run. I help loan officers speed up the doc-intake side of getting files committee-ready — worth me sending a quick example?","One thing I forgot to mention: the borrower-chase piece is usually the worst part — the ""still need your last two bank statements"" emails. We automate those nudges off whatever's missing from the file, so the borrower fills the gaps without you babysitting the inbox. On heavy-rehab files where the doc list runs long, that's where most of the time comes back. Want me to show you what that flow looks like on a sample multifamily file?","Last note from me, then I'll get out of your inbox. I know CIC runs to its own underwriting standard and Loan Committee format — this isn't about changing any of that. It just gets the borrower's docs sorted, named, and complete before they hit your standard, so you're verifying exceptions instead of building the stack from scratch each time. If you've got 15 minutes, I'd happily do a quick paperwork audit on your current intake and you keep whatever's useful.","We already have a process — CIC dictates its own file standard and runs in-house construction and closing departments, so an outside tool probably doesn't fit.","Totally fair, and I'm not trying to touch your standard or your closing department — those stay exactly as they are. This sits one step earlier: the borrower-doc intake before the file ever reaches your format. It sorts, renames, and files what the borrower sends, flags what's still missing against your checklist, and nudges them for it — so the package that lands on your desk is already complete and in your structure. You're still the one verifying it for Committee; you're just not assembling raw docs by hand on every file. If it doesn't slot cleanly into how CIC already works, it's not worth your time and I'll say so.","Person + title + company confirmed across multiple independent sources: Chaston Montgomery, MBA is Senior Loan Officer at Community Investment Corporation (his LinkedIn, TheOrg org-chart, RocketReach, CIC site). Prior roles: ELCA Mission Investment Fund, Urban Partnership Bank, Great Lakes Bank. | Closing cadence verified on his own LinkedIn activity: a 6-unit Beverly acquisition closing posted ~1 day ago, plus a 7-unit new-construction at 6601 S. Evans in Woodlawn posted ~3 weeks ago — the Woodlawn deal matches the recorded evidence almost exactly and confirms an active, recent run of closings. | Company shape confirmed: CIC is a US Treasury-certified CDFI and FHA multifamily lender, founded 1974, $1.8B+ deployed across ~69,000 units, focused on South/West Side rehab and Woodlawn new-construction. Single-lender originator, not a multi-lender broker. Lean team (LinkedIn lists 11-50 / ~80). | Active hiring confirmed: CIC careers page lists a Senior Loan Officer opening; the JD explicitly names 'developing loan packages for presentation to the CIC Loan Committee' as a core duty — documentation packaging is structurally central to the role and the team is staffing up against volume.","STRUCTURAL MISMATCH (primary risk): CIC is a single-lender CDFI; Chaston originates to one internal rulebook and presents to CIC's own Loan Committee. Lender/originator side, not the multi-lender broker who shops files across a panel — the exact shape the lead note flags as outside Oloxa's core. | Signal is celebration-shaped (closing wins he is proudly posting), not pain-shaped; a 'congrats' opener risks reading as flattery. Lead with the volume/packaging grind, not the win. | CIC dictates its own file standard and runs in-house construction/closing departments, so an external packaging tool may add limited value where the lender already controls format and pipeline — expect a 'we already have a process' objection. | The recorded '4 closings in 60 days' count is plausible and directionally supported but only two closings are independently confirmed publicly — do not quote the exact figure back to him as verified.",""
"20","Sway","US","HIRING","True","0-30 days","LOW","False","Jody Hill","Sr. Loan Officer","Lendmarq","jody.hill@lendmarq.com","https://www.linkedin.com/in/jody-hill-6124ba167","A direct lender scaling originators and adding underwriting/draws/coordinator headcount has to ingest, chase, and normalize messy borrower documents across four products at higher volume -- but Lendmarq is solving this by hiring in-house underwriting/processing, the opposite of outsourcing the lender-packaging step Oloxa sells to brokers.","The April 2026 Castlelake majority acquisition plus active origination + ops reqs make this a genuine 0-30-day scaling moment, and a new-hire LO is at peak openness to tools that speed file turn times -- but on the lender side of the transaction, not the broker side.","LinkedIn connect D0 (no pitch) -> LinkedIn DM D2 once accepted -> Email D4 (inbound-intake angle) -> Follow-up 1 email D8 (missing-docs/chase angle) -> Cold call D11 (permission-based) + voicemail if no answer -> Follow-up 2 email D15 (four-product turn-time angle, soft 15-min CTA). Lead with LinkedIn since a new-hire LO checks it often; hold SMS unless a mobile surfaces or the thread warms.","Hi Jody — saw the move into Lendmarq and the Castlelake news in April. Followed your Dunmor originations work too. Always curious how originators are handling file turn times across bridge/DSCR/fix&flip right now. Happy to connect.","Hi Jody — congrats on the Lendmarq move, and the Castlelake stake in April looks like it's pouring fuel on origination. Genuinely curious how the front-door borrower docs are holding up as volume climbs across four products at once — bridge, fix&flip, DSCR and multifamily each want a different stack, and at scale the intake/sorting/chasing piece tends to clog before underwriting even sees the file. We help lenders take that first messy pile from a borrower and get it sorted, renamed, filed, with missing items flagged automatically — so your team only touches exceptions. Not pitching you a way to package files OUT (you're the funder, I get that) — more the inbound side. Worth comparing notes?","Borrower docs across four products","Jody — congrats on the Lendmarq move.

With the Castlelake stake landing in April and originators ramping, the front-door borrower docs tend to pile up fast — especially spanning bridge, fix&flip, DSCR and multifamily, since each wants a different stack.

We take that first messy upload from a borrower and get it sorted, renamed, filed, with missing items flagged and the borrower nudged automatically — so your people only touch the exceptions, not the chase.

Not the lender-packaging angle — you're the funder. This is purely the inbound intake side.

Worth comparing notes?","Hi Jody, it's [name] — I know I'm calling out of the blue, can I borrow thirty seconds and you tell me if it's worth continuing? I saw you joined Lendmarq and that Castlelake took a majority stake back in April, so I figured originations were ramping hard right now. I wanted to ask how the inbound borrower docs are holding up across all four products as the volume climbs.","Hi Jody, it's [name] — reaching out because you're scaling originations at Lendmarq across four products and the front-door borrower docs tend to get messy fast at that pace. I've got a quick example of how lenders are sorting and flagging that intake before it hits underwriting — worth a look, my number's [number].","Hi Jody — [name] here. With Lendmarq ramping originations across bridge/DSCR/fix&flip, curious how the inbound borrower docs are holding up. Mind if I send over a quick example of how lenders are handling that intake?","New angle — the part that bites at scale isn't the docs you get, it's the ones you don't. When intake auto-flags what's missing and nudges the borrower directly, your team stops being the chase loop. With reqs open across both originators and the coordinator/ops side, that gap is usually where the time goes first as volume climbs. Worth me sending a quick example?","Last one from me. The reason I keep poking at the four-product thing: a borrower file for DSCR looks nothing like a fix&flip rehab draw package, and at higher volume the mis-sorted-at-intake problem quietly adds days to turn times. If file speed is a number you're watching this year, open to a 15-min look at how we tighten the front door?","We're hiring our own underwriting and processing team for this — we're building it in-house, not outsourcing it.","Makes total sense, and I'm not pitching against that — the in-house bench is the right call for underwriting judgment. The piece I'm talking about sits before they ever open the file: taking a borrower's raw, messy upload and getting it sorted, renamed, and missing-item-flagged so your new coordinators spend their time on decisions, not on renaming PDFs and chasing the borrower for the same paystub three times. It tends to make the team you're hiring go further, not replace it — especially while you're ramping headcount across four products at once.","Lendmarq has live, multi-board job postings for 'Loan Advisor' (Bridge/Fix&Flip/DSCR/multifamily) and a 'Senior Loan Officer' role (LinkedIn job 4376400722), plus ~10 open reqs incl. Loan Coordinator and Construction Draws Analyst -- hiring is real and current (verified across Rippling ATS, Zoho Recruit, Glassdoor, Teal, ZipRecruiter). | Fresh capital trigger: in April 2026 Castlelake acquired a majority stake in Lendmarq's parent (Resfin Partners) -- a datable event that explains the origination + ops hiring push better than the postings alone. | Jody Hill / Dunmor->Lendmarq move is consistent: a Jody Hill is listed at Dunmor (dunmor.com/sales-team-jhill, VP of Originations), and Dunmor is a confirmed fix&flip/DSCR/bridge/ground-up lender, so the title and product fluency are credible. | Lendmarq is a direct/private lender (~36 employees, Miami-based, fix&flip up to 90% LTC / 75% ARV), i.e. the funding party building its own origination/underwriting bench -- lender-side, not a broker packaging files out to lenders.","LENDER-SIDE, NOT BROKER-SIDE: Jody is a Sr. Loan Officer at the funding lender and the hiring is Lendmarq staffing its OWN underwriting/processing -- the opposite of Oloxa's broker-packaging ICP, and the in-house build arguably reduces the need Oloxa fills. | The hiring + capital event read as routine post-acquisition growth, not an acute document-intake pain Oloxa uniquely resolves -- HIRING is a soft trigger here. | Borrower docs flow TO Lendmarq as the lender; Oloxa's 'package a clean file out to multiple lenders' value prop is structurally misaligned with a single-destination direct lender. | The funded fix&flip terms (90% purchase / 100% rehab) are standard private-lender marketing, not evidence of operational strain. | Pitching broker file-packaging to a lender's own LO risks signaling you don't understand their side of the table -- if approached, the angle would have to pivot to high-volume inbound borrower-doc intake, not lender packaging.","follow_up_1 contains an unsupported claim: 'For a lender adding originators faster than coordinators, that gap usually shows up first.' The research evidence lists open reqs (Loan Advisor, Senior Loan Officer, Loan Coordinator, Construction Draws Analyst) but provides NO data on the relative hiring pace between originators and coordinators. Asserting originators are being added 'faster than coordinators' is a fabricated quantitative asymmetry about the prospect's org, which fails the 'no claim unsupported by evidence' rule. Fixed by re-grounding the line in what is actually known (hiring across both origination and coordination/ops, at higher volume across four products) while keeping the missing-docs/chase angle."
